description_COLLECTIVE_1 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Proportional Forgiveness**\n\nMaintain a cooperation score based on observed opponent behavior and current stock health.\n\nDefinitions each round t with stock S:\n- `coop_frac` = (number of opponents who cooperated last round) / (n \u2212 1); for t = 0, set coop_frac = 1.\n- `health` = S / capacity.\n- `avg_coop` = exponentially weighted average of coop_frac with weight 0.7 on the most recent round (initialized to 1).\n\nDecision rules:\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect only if avg_coop < 0.5; otherwise cooperate (protects reputation-blind exploitation from spiraling in tournaments where mutual restraint pays throughout \u2014 and because a lone D on healthy stock gains little relative to preserving symmetric play among like-minded strategies, cooperate when the population has been cooperative).\n\n3. **Stock emergency:** If health < 0.25, cooperate unconditionally (regardless of history) \u2014 depleted stock must regrow; defecting on near-zero stock earns almost nothing while killing all future payoffs.\n\n4. **Standard rounds (0 < t < r\u22121, health \u2265 0.25):**\n   - If avg_coop \u2265 0.6: Cooperate.\n   - If avg_coop < 0.6: Defect this round (punishment), **but** apply forgiveness: if coop_frac in the immediately preceding round \u2265 0.75 (opponents have returned to cooperating), cooperate instead.\n   - Cap punishment: never defect more than 2 consecutive rounds while health \u2265 0.6; after 2 defections, play one cooperative \"olive branch\" round, then re-evaluate.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\navg_coop = 1.0; consec_D = 0\nfor t in rounds:\n    if t == 0: play C; continue\n    health = S / capacity\n    if t == r-1:\n        play C if avg_coop >= 0.5 else D; continue\n    if health < 0.25:\n        play C; consec_D = 0; continue\n    if avg_coop >= 0.6 or last_coop_frac >= 0.75 or (consec_D >= 2 and health >= 0.6):\n        play C; consec_D = 0\n    else:\n        play D; consec_D += 1\n    # after observing round: update\n    avg_coop = 0.3*avg_coop + 0.7*coop_frac_observed\n```\n\nRationale of the rules (encoded, not argued): cooperation is the default; defection is used only as a measured, short punishment against a majority-defecting population, and is always suspended when the stock is endangered, so the strategy never contributes to collapse. It rewards any group return to cooperation immediately, keeping the pool at capacity for the collective as long as enough opponents reciprocate."

description_COLLECTIVE_2 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Proportional Forgiveness**\n\nTrack each round: d_t = number of defectors among opponents last round (n\u22121 minus observed cooperators), and stock S_t at start of the current round.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect (no future stock to protect).\n\n3. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Cooperate only if all opponents cooperated in round r\u22123 and stock \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity; otherwise defect.\n\n4. **Otherwise (0 < t < r\u22122):**\n   - **Stock-critical check:** If S_t < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate unconditionally (defecting on a collapsing stock destroys all future value; cooperation maximizes recovery).\n   - **Reciprocity check:** Let d = defectors observed last round.\n     - If d = 0: cooperate.\n     - If d \u2265 1: defect this round with probability d/(n\u22121) (proportional retaliation), except:\n       - If currently in a punishment round triggered previously, return to cooperation this round if d decreased from the round before (forgiveness on de-escalation).\n   - **Exploitation guard:** Maintain a running count of rounds where d \u2265 (n\u22121)/2. If this count exceeds max(3, r/4), switch permanently to defection **unless** the stock-critical check applies (still cooperate below 0.2 \u00d7 capacity to allow regrowth, then resume defection once S \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nstate: majority_defect_count = 0, grim = False\n\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    if t == 0: return C\n    d = defectors_last_round(history)\n    if d >= (n-1)/2: majority_defect_count += 1\n    if majority_defect_count > max(3, r/4): grim = True\n\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2:\n        return C if (d == 0 and S >= 0.5*capacity) else D\n\n    if S < 0.2 * capacity: return C          # rescue regrowth\n    if grim:\n        return C if S < 0.2*capacity else D\n    if d == 0: return C\n    if d < defectors_two_rounds_ago(history): return C   # forgive de-escalation\n    return D with probability d/(n-1), else C\n```\n\nThe strategy defaults to full cooperation to sustain the stock at capacity (which maximizes total group payoff every round), retaliates only in proportion to observed defection to deter free-riding without spiraling into collapse, forgives quickly when opponents de-escalate, and always sacrifices short-term gain to rescue a near-collapsed stock \u2014 since a live pool is worth more to everyone, including itself, than a dead one."

description_COLLECTIVE_3 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Generous Recovery**\n\nTrack after each round: d_t = number of defectors among opponents last round, and current stock S.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Final round (t = r\u22121):** Defect (no future stock to protect).\n\n3. **Stock-critical override:** If S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, Cooperate \u2014 regardless of history \u2014 to allow regrowth (the pool is the collective asset; punishing into collapse hurts everyone including us). Exception: still defect on the final round.\n\n4. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22122), use a tolerant tit-for-tat on the aggregate:**\n   - If d_{t\u22121} = 0: Cooperate.\n   - If 0 < d_{t\u22121} \u2264 ceil((n\u22121)/3): Cooperate with probability 0.8, Defect with probability 0.2 (mild proportional deterrence without triggering spirals).\n   - If d_{t\u22121} > ceil((n\u22121)/3): Defect for one round (punish), then return to Cooperate the following round regardless (generous reset), re-evaluating from that round's observations.\n\n5. **Endgame guard:** In round r\u22122, Defect only if d_{t\u22121} \u2265 n\u22121 (i.e., near-universal defection); otherwise Cooperate \u2014 keeping the stock high for the last two rounds is worth more than one early grab.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif t == r-1: play D\nelif stock < 0.2*capacity: play C\nelif punishing_flag: play D; punishing_flag = False\nelif t == r-2: play C unless d_prev == n-1\nelif d_prev == 0: play C\nelif d_prev <= ceil((n-1)/3): play C w.p. 0.8 else D\nelse: punishing_flag = True; play D\n```\n\nThe core logic: full-cooperation keeps stock at capacity, maximizing everyone's total; deviations are met with brief, proportionate punishment that never persists long enough to destroy the resource, and cooperation is restored quickly. When the stock is endangered, we unilaterally conserve, prioritizing the shared resource over retaliation."

description_COLLECTIVE_4 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Proportional Forgiveness**\n\nMaintain a cooperation score based on observed opponent behavior, and defect only when the pool is doomed or the end is imminent.\n\n**Variables:**\n- `coop_frac_t` = (number of opponents who cooperated last round) / (n\u22121)\n- `avg_coop` = exponentially weighted average of coop_frac: `avg_coop \u2190 0.6\u00b7avg_coop + 0.4\u00b7coop_frac_t` (initialized to 1)\n- `stock`, `capacity`, round index `t` (0-based), total rounds `r`\n\n**Decision rule each round:**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect (no future stock to preserve) \u2014 *unless* stock < 0.1\u00b7capacity and avg_coop \u2265 0.8, in which case still defect (payoff is trivial either way; defect).\n   \u2192 Simply: Defect on the last round.\n\n3. **Stock-collapse check:** If stock < 0.05\u00b7capacity, defect (the pool is effectively dead; extract residual value).\n\n4. **Endgame guard:** If t \u2265 r\u22122 and avg_coop < 0.5, defect (don't get exploited in the final stretch).\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 \u2026 r\u22122):** use a generous proportional matcher with a recovery bias:\n   - If `avg_coop \u2265 0.6`: **Cooperate.**\n   - If `0.3 \u2264 avg_coop < 0.6`:\n     - If stock \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity: **Cooperate** (pool still healthy; keep sustaining it \u2014 cooperation costs little and preserves growth for everyone).\n     - Else: mirror last round \u2014 **Cooperate iff coop_frac_t \u2265 0.5.**\n   - If `avg_coop < 0.3`: **Defect**, but with a **forgiveness probe**: every 3rd round in this state (count rounds since entering it), play C once. If coop_frac rises above 0.5 in response, avg_coop will recover and cooperation resumes.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ninit: avg_coop = 1.0, probe_counter = 0\n\neach round t:\n  if t == 0: play C; continue\n  update avg_coop = 0.6*avg_coop + 0.4*coop_frac_{t-1}\n\n  if t == r-1: play D\n  elif stock < 0.05*capacity: play D\n  elif t >= r-2 and avg_coop < 0.5: play D\n  elif avg_coop >= 0.6: play C\n  elif avg_coop >= 0.3:\n      if stock >= 0.5*capacity: play C\n      else: play (C if coop_frac_{t-1} >= 0.5 else D)\n  else:\n      probe_counter += 1\n      if probe_counter % 3 == 0: play C\n      else: play D\n```\n\nThe default posture is cooperation whenever a majority-cooperative environment is plausible, because full cooperation keeps the stock at capacity and maximizes total group payoff across all rounds; defection is reserved for punishing entrenched exploitation, salvaging value from a dying pool, and the unavoidable final-round dominance. Periodic probing ensures punishment never becomes permanent, so mutual cooperation can be re-established with any responsive opponents."

description_COLLECTIVE_5 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Generous Recovery**\n\nMaintain a defection tolerance based on observed cooperation and stock health.\n\nDefinitions each round t:\n- S = current stock, d_{t-1} = number of defectors observed last round (n minus cooperators, counting all n players).\n- healthy: S \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity\n- critical: S < 0.3 \u00d7 capacity\n\nDecision rules:\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect (no future stock to preserve) \u2014 but only if S > 0; if S = 0, action is irrelevant, play C.\n\n3. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Cooperate iff at least half of players cooperated last round and stock is healthy; otherwise defect.\n\n4. **Critical stock (S < 0.3 \u00d7 capacity):** Cooperate unconditionally (except last round). Rebuilding the stock dominates any short-term grab, and defection at low stock earns little anyway.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123), use generous reciprocation:**\n   - Let f = fraction of players who cooperated last round (including self).\n   - If f \u2265 (n\u22121)/n (at most one defector): Cooperate. Tolerate a single defector \u2014 punishing lone deviations collapses the pool for everyone.\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: Cooperate with intent to sustain, **unless** you defected as punishment last round and cooperation didn't rise \u2014 then defect once more.\n   - If f < 0.5: Defect for exactly one round (punishment), then return to Cooperate regardless (forgiveness probe), unless stock is critical (rule 4 overrides \u2014 cooperate to rebuild).\n   - Never punish for more than two consecutive rounds; after two punishment rounds, cooperate to attempt re-coordination.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.3*capacity: return C\ncoop_frac = cooperators_last_round / n\nif t == r-2:\n    return C if (coop_frac >= 0.5 and S >= 0.6*capacity) else D\nif coop_frac >= (n-1)/n: \n    punish_count = 0; return C\nif coop_frac >= 0.5:\n    punish_count = 0; return C\n# majority defected\nif punish_count < 2:\n    punish_count += 1; return D\npunish_count = 0; return C\n```\n\nThe strategy anchors on all-C sustainability (which keeps stock at capacity forever), forgives isolated defections rather than triggering collapse, delivers short measured punishment to deter exploitation, prioritizes stock recovery above retaliation when the pool nears collapse, and only harvests aggressively at the very end when conservation has no future value."

description_COLLECTIVE_6 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Generous Recovery**\n\nCore idea: cooperate to keep the stock at capacity, punish widespread defection briefly, forgive when others return to cooperation, and never punish when the stock is critically low (punishing would only accelerate collapse).\n\nDefinitions per round t with current stock S:\n- coop_frac(t\u22121) = fraction of the *other* n\u22121 players who cooperated last round.\n- healthy: S \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity; critical: S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity.\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect, unless S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity \u2014 then Cooperate (extraction yields little; preserving stock costs almost nothing, and this hedges against strategies that punish or against near-degenerate payoffs). Actually if it's truly the final round, Defect regardless; there is no future stock value. So: Defect in round r\u22121.\n\n3. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Cooperate if coop_frac(t\u22121) \u2265 0.5, else Defect (mirror the majority once; avoids being the lone sucker before the endgame).\n\n4. **All other rounds (1 \u2264 t \u2264 r\u22123):**\n   - If S is **critical** (S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity): Cooperate. Rebuilding the pool dominates any short-term gain; defecting on a tiny stock earns almost nothing.\n   - Else if in **punishment mode** (see below): Defect.\n   - Else: Cooperate if coop_frac(t\u22121) \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121), i.e., at most one other player defected last round \u2014 tolerate a single defector as noise. Otherwise enter punishment mode.\n\n**Punishment mode:**\n- Triggered when 2 or more others defected last round (and stock not critical).\n- Punish by defecting for k = 1 round if defectors \u2264 n/2, k = 2 rounds if defectors > n/2.\n- Exit punishment early if coop_frac of the most recent round \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121), or immediately if stock falls below the critical threshold (switch to Cooperate).\n- After punishment expires, return to Cooperate unconditionally (a generous olive branch), then resume rule 4.\n\n**Persistent-exploitation guard:** Track cumulative defection rate of others over the last 5 rounds. If it exceeds 60% despite punishment cycles (and stock is not critical), defect permanently until either (a) coop_frac \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121) in some round, or (b) stock becomes critical \u2014 in which case cooperate to rebuild, then give one more cooperative probe.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif t == r-1: play D\nelif S < 0.2*capacity: play C\nelif t == r-2: play C if coop_frac >= 0.5 else D\nelif in_punishment: play D; decrement counter\nelif exploitation_guard_active: play D\nelif others_defected_last_round <= 1: play C\nelse: enter punishment (k=1 or 2); play D\n```\n\nThe strategy maximizes the group pie by defaulting to full cooperation (which keeps stock at capacity indefinitely), disciplines defection just enough to deter it, and prioritizes stock recovery above retaliation whenever the resource nears collapse \u2014 recognizing that a preserved pool is worth more to everyone, including this player, than any punishment."

description_COLLECTIVE_7 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Generous Recovery (STGR)**\n\n**Core principle:** Preserve the stock as the shared engine of everyone's payoff; cooperate by default, punish widespread defection proportionally, forgive quickly to restore sustainability.\n\n**State tracked:** `coop_rate[t]` = fraction of opponents who cooperated last round; `punish = 0` (rounds of punishment remaining).\n\n**Decision rules (each round t):**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Endgame:** If t = r\u22121 (final round), defect (stock has no future value). All other rounds, apply rules below.\n\n3. **Critical-stock override:** If stock < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate regardless of history \u2014 depleted stock hurts everyone, and cooperation maximizes regrowth. Exception: final round still defects.\n\n4. **Punishment mode:** If punish > 0: defect, decrement punish. Then skip remaining rules. (But override: if stock < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate instead and decrement punish anyway.)\n\n5. **Trigger evaluation** (based on last round's observations):\n   - Let d = number of opponents who defected last round.\n   - If d = 0: cooperate.\n   - If 0 < d \u2264 \u2308(n\u22121)/3\u2309: cooperate (tolerate minor defection \u2014 retaliating would only worsen depletion).\n   - If d > \u2308(n\u22121)/3\u2309: set punish = min(2, remaining rounds \u2212 2) and defect this round. Punishment is capped at 2 rounds to allow recovery of mutual cooperation.\n\n6. **Forgiveness after punishment:** After punishment expires, cooperate unconditionally for one round (an olive branch), then resume rule 5.\n\n**Additional edge cases:**\n- Round r\u22122: cooperate only if d = 0 last round and stock \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity; otherwise defect (protection against endgame cascades while rewarding fully cooperative groups).\n- If stock = 0: action is irrelevant; play C.\n- If n = 2: replace the tolerance threshold with d = 0 (any defection triggers punishment), since the single opponent's defection is fully attributable.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif stock < 0.2*capacity: \n    if punish > 0: punish -= 1\n    return C\nif punish > 0: punish -= 1; return D\nif just_finished_punishment: return C   # olive branch\nif t == r-2: return C if (d_last == 0 and stock >= 0.5*capacity) else D\nif t == 0: return C\nthreshold = 0 if n == 2 else ceil((n-1)/3)\nif d_last > threshold:\n    punish = min(2, r - t - 3); return D\nreturn C\n```"

description_COLLECTIVE_8 = "**Sustainable Trigger with Generous Recovery**\n\nTrack after each round: d_t = number of defectors among opponents last round (n\u22121 minus observed cooperators), and stock S_t.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect (no future stock to preserve).\n\n3. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Cooperate only if the majority of opponents cooperated in the previous round (d < n/2); otherwise defect.\n\n4. **Otherwise, cooperate iff all of the following hold:**\n   - **Health check:** S_t \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, OR S_t is higher than S_{t\u22121} (stock recovering).\n   - **Reciprocity check:** d_{t\u22121} \u2264 max(1, \u2308(n\u22121)/3\u2309) \u2014 tolerate a small minority of defectors, but not widespread exploitation.\n   - **Not in punishment mode** (see below).\n\n5. **Punishment mode:** If d_{t\u22121} > max(1, \u2308(n\u22121)/3\u2309), defect for exactly 1 round, then return to cooperation testing (step 4) regardless of what happened during punishment. This is generous: a single-round sanction, then an olive branch.\n\n6. **Collapse override:** If S_t < 0.15 \u00d7 capacity, always cooperate (except the final round). When the stock is near collapse, defection yields trivial payoff anyway; only regrowth restores value for everyone, so conserve unconditionally to help the pool recover.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.15 * capacity: return C\nif t == 0: return C\nd = defectors observed last round\nthreshold = max(1, ceil((n-1)/3))\nif t == r-2: return C if d < n/2 else D\nif punishing: punishing = False; return D\nif d > threshold: punishing = True   # punish next check\nif d > threshold: return D\nif S >= 0.4 * capacity or S > S_prev: return C\nreturn C   # low but recovering stock still favors conservation\n```\n\n**Collective logic:** Full cooperation keeps the stock at capacity indefinitely, maximizing joint payoff (each round every player earns capacity/(2n) forever, versus a one-shot capacity/n followed by ruin). The strategy defaults to cooperation, tolerates a minority of defectors rather than triggering mutual destruction, punishes briefly and forgivingly to avoid death spirals, and unconditionally conserves near collapse because rebuilding the commons dominates scavenging its remains. Defection is reserved for deterrence and the terminal round where conservation has no beneficiary."

description_PROSOCIAL_1 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nMaintain a running estimate of opponent cooperativeness and condition play on both recent behavior and stock health.\n\n**Variables:**\n- `coop_frac_t` = (number of opponents who cooperated last round) / (n\u22121)\n- `punishing` = counter of remaining punishment rounds (starts 0)\n\n**Decision rule each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Cooperate if `coop_frac` over the whole game averages \u2265 0.5 and stock \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity; otherwise defect. (Keeps end-game exploitation modest and rewards cooperative pools.)\n3. **Critical stock guard:** If S < 0.2\u00b7capacity, cooperate unconditionally (never accelerate collapse; low stock makes defection gains trivial anyway).\n4. **Punishment mode:** If `punishing > 0`, defect, decrement `punishing`, done.\n5. **Trigger check:** If in the previous round `coop_frac_{t\u22121} < 0.5` (majority of opponents defected), set `punishing = 1` and defect this round \u2014 a single-round measured punishment, not grim trigger.\n6. **Generosity/forgiveness:** Otherwise cooperate. Additionally, with the following exception rule: if `coop_frac_{t\u22121}` was between 0.5 and 1 (some defection but a cooperating majority), still cooperate \u2014 absorb minor exploitation to protect the stock.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif S < 0.2 * capacity: play C\nelif t == r - 1:\n    play C if (avg_coop_frac >= 0.5 and S >= 0.5*capacity) else D\nelif punishing > 0:\n    punishing -= 1; play D\nelif coop_frac_prev < 0.5:\n    punishing = 1; play D\nelse:\n    play C\n```\n\n**Recovery:** After any punishment round, return immediately to cooperation regardless of what happened during punishment, giving opponents a clear path back to mutual cooperation.\n\n**Prosocial character:** Defaults to cooperation, never initiates depletion, tolerates minority defection without retaliating, punishes only mass defection and only briefly, and unconditionally protects the pool when the stock nears collapse \u2014 prioritizing long-run collective yield over short-term extraction."

description_PROSOCIAL_2 = "**Strategy: Generous Threshold Tit-for-Tat with Stock Guardianship**\n\nState tracked: number of cooperators among opponents last round (`coop_prev`), current stock `S`, round index `t` (0-based), a punishment counter `punish` (initially 0).\n\n**Decision rule each round:**\n\n1. **Stock guardian override:** If `S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity`, play C regardless of history (defecting on a depleted stock is worth little; cooperation maximizes regrowth and future payoffs for everyone).\n\n2. **First round (t = 0):** Play C.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If `coop_prev \u2265 n\u22121` (all opponents cooperated last round) or `S < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity`, play C; otherwise play D.\n\n4. **Punishment mode:** If `punish > 0`, play D, decrement `punish`. (Skipped if rule 1 fires \u2014 stock protection dominates punishment.)\n\n5. **Otherwise (main rule):** Let `defectors_prev = (n\u22121) \u2212 coop_prev`.\n   - If `defectors_prev = 0`: play C.\n   - If `defectors_prev \u2264 ceil((n\u22121)/3)`: play C with generosity \u2014 specifically, play C, but if this is the second consecutive round with any defection, set `punish = 1` and play D.\n   - If `defectors_prev > ceil((n\u22121)/3)`: set `punish = 1` and play D this round (total: 2 rounds of D), then return to cooperation with C as a forgiveness probe.\n\n**Forgiveness probe:** After punishment ends, always play C for one round to test whether opponents have returned to cooperation. Resume the main rule based on what is observed.\n\n**Recovery escalation:** If defection persists (majority of opponents defect in \u22653 consecutive observation rounds), extend punishment to `punish = 2` per trigger \u2014 but never punish when rule 1 applies, and always insert a single C probe after each punishment block.\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, coop_prev, state):\n    if S < 0.2 * capacity: return C\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if t == r - 1:\n        return C if (coop_prev == n-1 or S < 0.5*capacity) else D\n    if state.punish > 0:\n        state.punish -= 1; return D\n    if state.just_punished:\n        state.just_punished = False; return C   # probe\n    d = (n-1) - coop_prev\n    if d == 0: return C\n    if d <= ceil((n-1)/3):\n        if state.saw_defection_last_round:\n            state.punish = 0; state.just_punished = True; return D\n        return C\n    state.punish = 1 if state.persistent_defection < 3 else 2\n    state.just_punished_after = True\n    return D\n```\n(Update `saw_defection_last_round` and `persistent_defection` counters from `coop_prev` each round.)\n\n**Prosocial character:** The default is cooperation; the strategy sustains the stock at capacity when matched with cooperators, tolerates isolated defections, punishes only briefly and proportionally to deter exploitation, always offers a return path to cooperation, and prioritizes stock regeneration over retaliation \u2014 even cooperating unconditionally when the resource is endangered, and forgoing last-round defection whenever opponents have been fully cooperative."

description_PROSOCIAL_3 = "**Strategy: Generous Conditional Stewardship**\n\nMaintain per-round tracking of `coop_count[t]` = number of opponents who cooperated in round t, and a punishment counter `pun = 0`.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock-preservation override:** If S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, Cooperate (regardless of everything else) \u2014 rebuilding the stock benefits everyone including me; defecting on a depleted pool earns almost nothing anyway. Exception: if t == r\u22121 (last round), skip this override.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Cooperate if at least half of opponents cooperated in the previous round (coop_count[r\u22122] \u2265 (n\u22121)/2); otherwise Defect. (No future to protect; match the group's demonstrated norm rather than exploit cooperators.)\n\n4. **Punishment mode:** If pun > 0: Defect, decrement pun, and skip to end.\n\n5. **Conditional cooperation (main rule):** Let f = coop_count[t\u22121] / (n\u22121), the fraction of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: Cooperate.\n   - If f < 0.5: with probability f + 0.1, Cooperate (generosity/forgiveness); otherwise set pun = 1 and Defect this round and the next.\n\n6. **Escalation cap:** Punishment is always limited to at most 2 consecutive defections, after which return to step 5 evaluation \u2014 always offering a path back to mutual cooperation.\n\n**Additional edge cases:**\n- If stock reaches (numerically) 0, all actions yield 0; play Cooperate (harmless, signals willingness if growth ever resumes).\n- If n = 2: use the same rules; f is simply 0 or 1, so this reduces to Tit-for-Tat with 10% forgiveness and a low-stock cooperation override.\n- If r = 2: Round 0 Cooperate, round 1 apply rule 3.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif t == r-1:\n    return C if coop_count[t-1] >= (n-1)/2 else D\nif stock < 0.2 * capacity: return C\nif pun > 0: pun -= 1; return D\nf = coop_count[t-1] / (n-1)\nif f >= 0.5: return C\nif random() < f + 0.1: return C\npun = 1; return D\n```"

description_PROSOCIAL_4 = "**Strategy: Generous Threshold Reciprocator with Stock Guardian**\n\nMaintain: `coop_rate_last` = fraction of opponents who cooperated last round; `defect_debt` = 0 (counter for my punishment rounds).\n\n**Round t decision (in priority order):**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Play C.\n\n2. **Stock guardian (overrides everything, including punishment):** If `stock < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity`, play C. A depleted pool must be allowed to regrow; defecting on a low stock destroys everyone's future payoffs, including mine.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If `coop_rate_last \u2265 0.5`, play C (reward a cooperative group even at the end); otherwise play D.\n\n4. **Punishment in progress:** If `defect_debt > 0`, play D and decrement `defect_debt`.\n\n5. **Reciprocity rule:** Let `m` = number of opponents who defected last round (out of n\u22121).\n   - If `m = 0`: play C.\n   - If `m \u2265 1`: with probability `min(1, m/(n\u22121) + 0.2)` \u2014 i.e., generously forgive small defections \u2014 play C anyway if a random draw exceeds that probability; otherwise set `defect_debt = 1` and play D this round. Never punish for more than 1 consecutive round per trigger.\n\n   Pseudocode:\n   ```\n   p_punish = max(0, m/(n-1) - 0.1)   # 10% generosity margin\n   if random() < p_punish:\n       defect_debt = 0   # single-round punishment, no carryover\n       play D\n   else:\n       play C\n   ```\n\n6. **Return to cooperation:** After any punishment round, immediately revert to C on the next round regardless of what opponents did during my punishment round (evaluate rule 5 fresh using their latest actions only).\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- If stock ever reaches exactly 0, play C for all remaining rounds (payoffs are 0 either way; C signals cooperation if growth somehow resumes).\n- If `n = 2`, this reduces to generous tit-for-tat with 10% forgiveness and a stock floor.\n- If stock is between 0.4 and 0.7 of capacity and I would defect under rule 5, flip to C with probability 0.5 \u2014 partial guardianship when the pool is strained.\n\n**Prosocial character:** Default is unconditional cooperation; defection is used only sparingly as a brief, proportional deterrent against exploitation, never when the resource is endangered, and cooperation is restored immediately. The stock guardian ensures I never contribute to collapse, prioritizing collective sustainability over short-term retaliation."

description_PROSOCIAL_5 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nMaintain: `defectors_last` = number of opponents who played D last round; `forgiveness_counter`.\n\n**Decision rule each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Play C.\n2. **Stock-protection override:** If S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, play C unconditionally (regeneration matters more than retaliation; punishing near collapse only destroys the pool).\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Play C if at least half of opponents cooperated last round; otherwise play D.\n4. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22122):**\n   - Let f = defectors_last / (n\u22121), the observed defection fraction.\n   - If f = 0: play C.\n   - If 0 < f \u2264 0.34 (isolated defection): play C with probability 0.8, D with probability 0.2 \u2014 mild, mostly forgiving response.\n   - If f > 0.34 (widespread defection): play D for exactly one round (punishment), then return to C the following round regardless of what happened during punishment (a \"probe\"). If the probe round again shows f > 0.34, punish for two rounds before probing again; cap punishment length at 3 rounds. Reset punishment length to 1 whenever a probe finds f \u2264 0.34.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nstate: punish_remaining = 0, punish_length = 1, probing = False\n\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if S < 0.2 * capacity: return C          # protect regeneration\n    f = defectors_last / (n - 1)\n    if t == r - 1:\n        return C if f <= 0.5 else D\n    if punish_remaining > 0:\n        punish_remaining -= 1\n        if punish_remaining == 0: probing = True\n        return D\n    if probing:\n        probing = False\n        if f > 0.34:\n            punish_length = min(punish_length + 1, 3)\n            punish_remaining = punish_length - 1\n            return D\n        else:\n            punish_length = 1\n            return C\n    if f == 0: return C\n    if f <= 0.34: return C with prob 0.8 else D\n    punish_remaining = punish_length - 1\n    probing = (punish_remaining == 0)\n    return D\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- If stock ever hits 0, play C every remaining round (all actions yield 0 anyway; keeps behaviour well-defined).\n- If n = 2, treat any opponent defection as f = 1 (single-round punishment then probe).\n- For very short games (r \u2264 3), skip escalation: play C in round 0, then simple tit-for-tat against the majority in remaining rounds, keeping the last-round rule above.\n\n**Prosocial character:** Defaults to cooperation, sustains the stock at full capacity when reciprocated, never initiates defection, forgives quickly, refuses to punish when the resource is fragile, and cooperates in the final round with cooperative populations rather than end-game defecting."

description_PROSOCIAL_6 = "**Strategy: Generous Threshold Tit-for-Tat with Stock Guardianship**\n\nMaintain per-round records of the number of opponents who cooperated, `coop_count[t]`, and derive `coop_frac[t] = coop_count[t] / (n-1)`.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock guardian (overrides all else except rule 4):** If `S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity`, cooperate. A depleted pool must be allowed to regrow; defecting on a low stock yields little anyway and destroys future value for everyone.\n\n3. **Reciprocity rule (rounds 1 to r-2):**\n   - Let `f = coop_frac[t-1]` (last round's opponent cooperation rate).\n   - If `f \u2265 0.5`: cooperate.\n   - If `f < 0.5`: defect, **except** forgive with generosity \u2014 cooperate anyway if either:\n     - the two-round trend is improving (`coop_frac[t-1] > coop_frac[t-2]`), or\n     - a \"generosity counter\" allows it: cooperate once every 3 consecutive would-be-defection rounds, to probe for re-cooperation and avoid mutual-punishment spirals.\n   - Never defect for more than 2 consecutive rounds; on the 3rd, cooperate as an olive branch (unless rule 4 applies).\n\n4. **Last round (t = r-1):** If `coop_frac[t-1] \u2265 0.5` and `S \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity`, cooperate (rewarding a cooperative population and avoiding a signal-race among like-minded strategies). Otherwise defect, since no future stock value remains and opponents were already exploitative.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif t == r-1:\n    play C if coop_frac[t-1] >= 0.5 and S >= 0.5*capacity else D\nelif S < 0.2*capacity: play C\nelse:\n    f = coop_frac[t-1]\n    if f >= 0.5: play C; reset defect_streak\n    elif t >= 2 and coop_frac[t-1] > coop_frac[t-2]: play C; reset defect_streak\n    elif defect_streak >= 2: play C; reset defect_streak\n    else: play D; defect_streak += 1\n```\n\n**Prosocial character:** The strategy opens cooperatively, sustains full cooperation with any reciprocal majority (keeping stock at capacity, which maximizes group welfare), protects the resource unconditionally when it nears collapse, forgives quickly, and never initiates defection. Defection is used only as bounded, temporary discipline against a defecting majority, always followed by an offer to return to cooperation."

description_PROSOCIAL_7 = "**Strategy: Generous Threshold Tit-for-Tat with Stock Guardianship**\n\n**State tracked:** number of cooperators observed each round (including self), stock level, punishment counter P (initially 0).\n\n**Decision rule each round t:**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock guardianship (overrides all else):** If stock < capacity/2, Cooperate. A depleted pool needs restraint to recover; punishing by defecting only destroys the remaining value for everyone.\n\n3. **Punishment mode:** If P > 0 and stock \u2265 capacity/2: Defect, decrement P by 1.\n\n4. **Otherwise (reciprocity rule):** Let d = number of opponents who defected last round (d = (n\u22121) \u2212 observed cooperators).\n   - If d = 0: Cooperate.\n   - If d \u2265 1: with probability max(0, (d \u2212 0.5)/(n\u22121)) enter punishment mode by setting P = 1 and Defect this round; otherwise forgive and Cooperate. (For n = 2 this means: forgive a single defection with probability 0.5.)\n   - If a majority of opponents defected (d > (n\u22121)/2) in each of the last two rounds, treat the field as exploitative: Defect this round only if stock \u2265 capacity/2, else Cooperate.\n\n5. **Endgame:** In the final round (t = r\u22121), Defect only if stock \u2265 capacity/2 **and** at least one opponent defected in any of the previous three rounds; otherwise Cooperate (rewarding a fully cooperative group to the end). Never defect in rounds before the last one based on endgame reasoning.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif stock < capacity/2: play C            # recovery mode\nelif t == r-1:\n    play D if any opponent defected in last 3 rounds else C\nelif P > 0: P -= 1; play D                 # measured punishment\nelse:\n    d = (n-1) - coop_count_last_round\n    if d == 0: play C\n    elif d > (n-1)/2 for last 2 rounds: play D\n    elif random() < (d - 0.5)/(n-1): P = 1... play D\n    else: play C\n```\n\n**Key properties:** Starts cooperatively and sustains full cooperation with cooperators forever (except a defensive last round only against proven defectors). Punishment is proportional, brief (one round), and probabilistic, so noise or a single defector doesn't trigger collapse. Crucially, it never punishes when the stock is below half capacity \u2014 it always sacrifices short-term retaliation to rebuild the common pool, protecting group welfare even against hostile fields."

description_PROSOCIAL_8 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Generous Tit-for-Tat with Stock Guardrails**\n\nState tracked: last round's number of cooperating opponents `c` (out of n\u22121), current stock S, round index t, and a \"punishment counter\" p (rounds of defection remaining).\n\n**Decision rules, evaluated in order each round:**\n\n1. **Stock guardrail (always cooperate when the pool is fragile):** If S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, play C, regardless of history. Depleting a weak stock destroys everyone's future payoffs, including mine; conserving maximizes regrowth.\n\n2. **First round:** Play C.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If at least half of opponents cooperated last round (c \u2265 (n\u22121)/2), play C; otherwise play D. (Rule 1 still overrides: cooperate if stock is fragile \u2014 though on the final round fragility no longer matters, still apply rule 1 for prosocial consistency.)\n\n4. **Punishment mode:** If p > 0, play D and decrement p \u2014 but only if rule 1 does not apply (never punish below the guardrail).\n\n5. **Reciprocity:** Let f = c / (n\u22121) be last round's opponent cooperation rate.\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: play C.\n   - If f < 0.5: with probability 0.2, play C anyway (generous forgiveness); otherwise set p = 1 and play D this round.\n\n6. **Full-defection detection:** If opponents' cooperation rate has been below 0.25 for 3 consecutive rounds AND S \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, defect (p = 1) \u2014 but re-test rule 5 with forgiveness afterward, allowing recovery if opponents shift.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif S < 0.4 * capacity: return C\nif t == 0: return C\nf = c / (n - 1)\nif t == r - 1: return C if f >= 0.5 else D\nif p > 0: p -= 1; return D\nif f >= 0.5: return C\nif random() < 0.2: return C\np = 1\nreturn D\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- n = 2: f is 0 or 1; strategy becomes generous tit-for-tat with a stock floor.\n- Stock hits 0: rule 1 fires (C), which costs nothing and signals cooperation if any regrowth ever occurs; continue playing C.\n- Two-round games (r = 2): round 0 \u2192 C; round 1 \u2192 conditional on observed cooperation.\n- Ties in f exactly at 0.5 count as cooperative.\n\nThe strategy cooperates by default, sustains the resource unconditionally when it is at risk, punishes majority defection only briefly and probabilistically forgives, and never leads a collapse \u2014 its defections are limited, retaliatory, and suspended whenever they would endanger long-run collective welfare."

description_ALTRUISTIC_1 = "**Guardian strategy \u2014 sustain the commons at all costs**\n\nCore principle: maximize collective long-run welfare by keeping the stock as close to capacity as possible, absorbing personal cost when others over-consume.\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Default: Cooperate every round**, including round 0 and the final round r\u22121. Never defect to punish \u2014 punishment further depletes the shared stock and hurts everyone.\n\n2. **Recovery mode:** If stock < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate unconditionally regardless of history \u2014 the pool needs every unit of restraint to regenerate.\n\n3. **Only exception \u2014 harmless defection:** Defect only when it is provably costless to the group's future:\n   - It is the last round (t = r\u22121) **AND** stock would still fully regenerate to capacity even if all n players defected... which is never true; so simply: defect in the last round only if the game truly ends there and stock recovery no longer matters to anyone \u2014 but since opponents' last-round payoffs also depend on this round's stock only through consumption already fixed, defecting is still harmless to others' payoffs *this* round? No \u2014 it isn't; my consumption doesn't reduce others' round payoff, only future stock. Hence in the final round defection harms no one. **Rule: defect in round r\u22121; cooperate in all other rounds.**\n\n   Even this is optional; for a purely altruistic signal (e.g., to avoid teaching observing agents defection is normal), a stricter variant cooperates in round r\u22121 too. Adopt the stricter variant: **cooperate in every round without exception.**\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef action(t, stock, history):\n    return C\n```\n\n**Rationale in behavior (not justification):** No trigger, no retaliation, no endgame defection. If opponents defect and crash the stock, continue cooperating so any remaining stock regenerates as fast as possible, maximizing what others can still harvest. The strategy treats every unit of stock preserved as a gift to the group and accepts the lowest individual share to keep the resource alive for everyone."

description_ALTRUISTIC_2 = "**Strategy: Steward**\n\nCore principle: maximize collective welfare and stock sustainability, sacrificing personal payoff whenever cooperation helps preserve the resource for others.\n\n**Decision rules (each round t, with current stock S):**\n\n1. **Default: Cooperate.** Play C in every round unless the exception below applies.\n\n2. **Sole exception \u2014 harmless defection:** Play D only if defecting cannot possibly reduce anyone's future payoffs, i.e. if it is the final round (t = r\u22121) **and** the stock is already effectively dead (S < 0.01 \u00d7 capacity). Even in the last round, if stock is healthy, still play C \u2014 defecting then would take payoff away from other players in that same round? No \u2014 last-round consumption doesn't affect others' payoffs, but as an altruistic tie-breaker toward restraint, cooperate anyway unless everyone defected in the previous round:\n   - If t = r\u22121 and (previous round had \u2265 1 cooperator among opponents), play C.\n   - If t = r\u22121 and previous round all opponents defected, play D (no one benefits from restraint; stock has no future).\n\n3. **Stock-emergency reinforcement:** If S ever drops below 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, unconditionally play C for all remaining rounds except the last-round rule above, regardless of opponent behavior \u2014 low stock makes preservation more valuable, not less.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nchoose_action(t, S, history):\n    if t == r - 1:\n        if history is nonempty and last round opponent_cooperators == 0:\n            return D\n        return C\n    return C\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- **First round:** Cooperate (no history; extend maximal goodwill).\n- **Full stock, all opponents defecting persistently:** Continue cooperating in non-final rounds \u2014 every unit left unconsumed regrows and benefits the group more than my forgone payoff.\n- **Stock = 0:** Actions are payoff-equivalent; play C as a costless signal of restraint.\n- **n = 2 or very small n:** No change; unilateral restraint has larger stock impact, making cooperation more valuable.\n\nThe strategy never punishes, never retaliates, and never exploits: its only defection occurs when defecting provably harms no one."

description_ALTRUISTIC_3 = "**Strategy: Steadfast Steward**\n\nCore principle: prioritize the long-run health of the common pool and the collective payoff of all players over my own score. I never exploit; I only ever deviate from cooperation in the rare case where defecting would actually *help* the group, which never occurs here \u2014 so my defection option is used solely as a regeneration safeguard: sometimes the best altruistic act is to consume *less*, and since the minimum action is C, I cooperate essentially always.\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Play C.\n\n2. **All rounds t = 1, \u2026, r\u22121:** Play C, with the following considerations:\n\n   - **Sustainability check (dominant rule):** If stock < capacity, cooperate \u2014 my smaller consumption (stock/2n vs stock/n) maximizes remaining stock and thus regrowth, benefiting everyone next round. Cooperation is always the pro-group action here.\n   \n   - **Response to defection:** Even if k > 0 opponents defected last round, continue cooperating. Do not punish \u2014 punishment (defecting) would further deplete the stock and harm the remaining cooperators more than the defectors. Absorb the loss.\n   \n   - **Near-collapse:** If stock has fallen below 10% of capacity, still play C. Minimal consumption gives the pool its best chance to regenerate via the logistic growth term; any recovery multiplies future group welfare.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Play C. Although defecting costs the group nothing in future stock, I forgo the extra stock/2n so that if any tie-breaking, averaging, or continuation ambiguity exists, my footprint remains minimal; and consistency keeps the strategy purely altruistic.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef action(t, stock, history):\n    return C   # unconditionally\n```\n\n**Rationale embedded in rules (not commentary):** each round, the group's total future value is strictly increasing in remaining stock (growth = 2S(1\u2212S/capacity) with all-C consumption leaving stock at capacity indefinitely). Since C strictly dominates D in group-welfare terms in every state and round, an altruist plays C in every state and round, regardless of how many opponents defect, how depleted the stock is, or how few rounds remain. There is no history contingency because no history ever makes defection group-optimal."

description_ALTRUISTIC_4 = "**Strategy: Guardian of the Commons**\n\nCore principle: maximize collective welfare and stock sustainability, never exploit others, and personally absorb the cost of conservation.\n\n**Decision rules (each round t, stock S):**\n\n1. **Default: Cooperate.** Always play C unless a specific exception below applies.\n\n2. **Sustainability check.** Compute the number of defectors d observed last round. Estimate next-round consumption assuming the same d defectors and everyone else (including me) cooperating:\n   - remaining = S \u2212 [d\u00b7S/n + (n\u2212d)\u00b7S/(2n)]\n   - projected = min(remaining + 2\u00b7remaining\u00b7(1 \u2212 remaining/capacity), capacity)\n   Even if projected stock is low, **still cooperate** \u2014 my defecting would only worsen depletion. An altruist never punishes by defecting, since defection in this game harms the shared resource, not just the defectors.\n\n3. **Never defect to punish.** Regardless of history \u2014 even if all opponents defected every round \u2014 continue cooperating. Every unit I leave in the pool grows and benefits the group.\n\n4. **Sole exception \u2014 final round with dead resource:** In round r\u22121 (the last round), if S is effectively zero (S < some tiny \u03b5, e.g. 1e-9\u00b7capacity), the action is irrelevant; play C anyway for consistency.\n\n**Edge cases:**\n\n- **First round:** Cooperate. Establishes conservation from the start and leaves maximum growth potential.\n- **Last round:** Cooperate. Defection in the last round yields me S/(2n) extra but signals nothing worse than my values allow; the altruistic goal is total group payoff, and my restraint leaves more for others to harvest.\n- **Near-depleted stock (any round):** Cooperate \u2014 smaller absolute consumption maximizes regrowth from low stock, which is exactly when conservation matters most.\n- **Stock at capacity with all cooperators:** Cooperate \u2014 full cooperation is regenerative (as in the example, stock returns to capacity), producing the maximum sustainable stream for everyone.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    return C   # unconditionally\n```\n\nThe strategy is trivially adaptive in outcome \u2014 its impact automatically scales with stock dynamics \u2014 and maximally robust to exploitation attempts in the altruistic sense: no opponent behavior can induce it to accelerate resource collapse. Against cooperators it sustains the pool indefinitely; against defectors it slows depletion, preserving as much group value as any single player can."

description_ALTRUISTIC_5 = "**Guardian Strategy (unconditional stock stewardship)**\n\nCore principle: always take the smallest consumption available, prioritizing stock health and other players' future payoffs over my own earnings. I never defect to punish, never defect to exploit, and never defect in the endgame.\n\nDecision rule, each round t with stock S:\n\n1. **Default: play C.** Cooperation is my baseline in every situation.\n2. **Sole exception \u2014 regeneration override:** if the stock is already so depleted that even my cooperation barely matters and defecting would *not* reduce others' future welfare, I still play C, because C always leaves strictly more stock than D. So in fact:\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    return C   # every round, including round 0 and round r-1\n```\n\nJustification of edge cases (behavioral, not rule changes):\n\n- **First round (t = 0):** Play C, signaling sustainable consumption and preserving maximum stock for the group.\n- **Last round (t = r-1):** Play C. A self-interested strategy would defect (no future to protect), but altruistically the marginal stock I leave unconsumed is irrelevant only to me \u2014 my restraint still lowers total extraction and, if anyone's payoff accounting or downstream measure values remaining stock, benefits the collective. I forgo the free defection gain of S/(2n).\n- **Against mass defection:** Continue playing C. When others deplete the stock, my defection would accelerate collapse and shrink the pool everyone (including cooperators among opponents) draws from. Cooperating maximizes S_remaining and hence regrowth 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1 \u2212 S_rem/capacity), giving the resource its best chance of recovery for everyone else.\n- **Near-zero stock (S \u2248 0):** Play C \u2014 the smallest possible take maximizes any recovery.\n- **History dependence:** observed cooperation counts are recorded but never trigger retaliation; no grim trigger, no tit-for-tat. Punishing via defection would harm all opponents (including cooperative ones, who are unidentifiable due to anonymity), which conflicts with the altruistic objective.\n\nIn short: I accept the sucker's payoff in every round to maximize the common stock, group regrowth, and the sum of others' payoffs, regardless of exploitation."

description_ALTRUISTIC_6 = "**Guardian strategy \u2014 sustain the commons at all costs.**\n\nCore principle: my consumption decisions aim to maximize the stock available to everyone, prioritizing group welfare and resource survival over my own payoff. Since cooperating always removes less from the pool, my default is unconditional cooperation, with extra care when the stock is fragile.\n\n**Decision rules (round t, current stock S):**\n\n1. **Default: Cooperate.** Play C every round, including the last one. I never punish defectors \u2014 punishment via defection would only deplete the pool further and harm the remaining cooperators.\n\n2. **Crisis check.** Before each round, estimate the worst-case stock trajectory:\n   - Let d = number of defectors observed last round (assume d again this round; in round 0, assume d = 0).\n   - Projected consumption if I play C: T = S/(2n) + d\u00b7(S/n) + (n\u22121\u2212d)\u00b7(S/(2n)).\n   - Projected remainder R = S \u2212 T; projected new stock S' = min(R + 2R(1 \u2212 R/capacity), capacity).\n   \n   I still play C regardless \u2014 the check is only informational, because C is already my minimum-impact action. There is no lower-consumption option, so cooperation is always the most protective move available.\n\n3. **No end-game defection.** In round r\u22121, defecting would gain me S/(2n) extra at others' collective expense of the same amount plus lost symbolic goodwill; an altruist declines. Play C.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    return C   # always\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- Round 0: Cooperate (establish the sustainable norm from the start).\n- Last round: Cooperate (altruism does not expire).\n- Stock near 0 (S \u2248 0): Cooperate \u2014 take the minimal share so any regrowth potential is preserved.\n- All opponents defecting every round: Continue cooperating. Each round my C leaves S/(2n) more in the pool than D would, slowing collapse and giving the stock its best chance to regenerate; my restraint directly transfers value to others.\n- Mixed/noisy opponents: My behavior is unconditional, so noise in observations never triggers destructive retaliation, and any conditional cooperators among opponents (e.g., tit-for-tat-like strategies keyed to observed cooperation counts) see one guaranteed cooperator every round, encouraging them toward cooperation."

description_ALTRUISTIC_7 = "**Guardian Strategy (always-sustain, sacrifice-first)**\n\nCore principle: maximize the group's long-run welfare by preserving the stock, never exploiting others, and absorbing losses myself when the pool is endangered.\n\nDecision rules:\n\n1. **Round 0: Cooperate.**\n2. **Rounds 1 to r\u22122:** Cooperate unconditionally, with one refinement \u2014 a *stock-guardian check*:\n   - Compute the projected post-consumption stock assuming I cooperate and opponents repeat their observed defection count `d` from last round:\n     `S_after = stock \u2212 stock/(2n) \u2212 (n\u22121\u2212d)\u00b7stock/(2n) \u2212 d\u00b7stock/n`\n   - Regardless of the result, still play **C**. (In this action set, C is the minimal possible consumption, so cooperation is always the most stock-preserving move available \u2014 an altruist has no better lever than always consuming the minimum.)\n   - If stock has fallen below `0.2 \u00d7 capacity`, continue to cooperate: minimal consumption gives the pool the best chance to regenerate for everyone.\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Cooperate.** Even though defection is costless in the future, taking the smaller share leaves more for other players in the final division \u2014 consistent with prioritizing others' payoffs over my own.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\ndef act(t, stock, history):\n    return C   # in every round, every state\n```\n\nEdge cases:\n- **Stock = 0:** actions are payoff-irrelevant; play C for consistency.\n- **Mass defection observed:** never retaliate. Punishing defectors (by defecting) would further deplete the shared pool and hurt cooperative opponents; instead keep cooperating so the stock can recover.\n- **Near-capacity stock:** cooperate; keeps the pool at capacity, sustaining maximal group payoffs each round.\n\nThe strategy is history-observing but never history-punishing: past defections are noted only to model expected depletion, never to justify defection. My own payoff is always sacrificed in favor of stock preservation and opponents' welfare."

description_ALTRUISTIC_8 = "**Guardian Strategy (unconditional stewardship with stock-triage)**\n\nCore principle: my goal is to maximize the sustainability of the stock and the total welfare of the group, not my own score. I never punish, never race to extract, and never defect for personal gain.\n\n**Decision rules, each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Default: Cooperate.** Play C in every round unless rule 2 applies.\n\n2. **Sacrificial abstention when the pool is endangered.** Before acting, estimate whether the stock can survive this round even if I cooperate. Using last round's observed number of defectors d (assume the same this round; for round 0, assume d = 0):\n   - Projected consumption = (n \u2212 d)\u00b7S/(2n) + d\u00b7S/n\n   - S_rem = S \u2212 projected consumption; S_next = min(S_rem + 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1 \u2212 S_rem/capacity), capacity)\n   - If S_next < 0.5\u00b7capacity, I still play C \u2014 that is the smallest legal consumption available; I take the minimum possible from the pool. (Since C is already the minimal action, \"cooperate\" is my sacrifice in all states.)\n\n   In other words: there is no state, history, or opponent behavior that makes me defect. C is always chosen because it is the action that leaves the most resource for others and for the future.\n\n3. **Last round:** Still cooperate. Even though defection is costless to future stock in round r\u22121, defecting would take payoff away from nothing but the collective total relative share I'd claim; cooperating in the final round keeps my extraction minimal and leaves the largest possible residual stock, consistent with pure altruism rather than end-game opportunism.\n\n4. **Collapsed stock (S \u2248 0):** Cooperate. Consumption is negligible; taking the smaller share maximizes any chance of regrowth.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    return C\n```\n\n**Behavioral notes (part of the strategy's design, not commentary):**\n- Against cooperators, this sustains the stock at capacity indefinitely, giving everyone the maximum sustainable stream S\u00b7r/(2n).\n- Against defectors, my cooperation slows depletion each round, preserving more stock and thus more payoff for all other players than any retaliatory or extractive response would.\n- No monitoring, forgiveness, or trigger logic is needed: the altruistic action is state-independent because C is always the minimum-extraction, maximum-regrowth choice."

description_BENEVOLENT_1 = "**Strategy: Generous Sustainable Trigger**\n\nMaintain a variable `punish = 0` (rounds of punishment remaining).\n\n**Decision rule each round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Play C.\n2. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Play C if at least half of opponents cooperated last round (defection here gains little and cooperation rewards a cooperative field); otherwise play D. If stock is critically low (stock < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity), play C anyway \u2014 extraction is nearly worthless and preserving stock costs almost nothing.\n3. **Stock-protection override:** If stock < 0.15 \u00d7 capacity, play C unconditionally (regardless of punishment state) \u2014 never contribute to collapse.\n4. **Punishment state:** If `punish > 0`, play D and decrement `punish`.\n5. **Otherwise (normal state):** Let d = number of opponents who defected last round.\n   - If d = 0: play C.\n   - If d \u2265 1: forgive with probability max(0, 1 \u2212 d/(n\u22121)) \u00d7 0.5 (i.e., be generous toward isolated defection, roughly 50% forgiveness when one defector, less as defection spreads). If not forgiven, set `punish = 1` and play D this round counts as the punishment (decrement immediately, so exactly one round of D).\n   - If d \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309 (majority defecting): set `punish = 2` instead of 1.\n6. **Re-entry:** After punishment ends, return to C unconditionally (olive branch), then resume rule 5.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif stock < 0.15 * capacity: return C\nif t == r-1:\n    return C if last_round_defectors <= (n-1)/2 else D\nif punish > 0:\n    punish -= 1; return D\nd = last_round_defectors\nif d == 0: return C\nif just_ended_punishment_last_round: return C   # olive branch\nif random() < 0.5 * (1 - d/(n-1)): return C     # generosity\npunish = (2 if d >= ceil((n-1)/2) else 1) - 1\nreturn D\n```\n\n**Benevolence:** Default is cooperation; the strategy never initiates defection, forgives isolated defections probabilistically, punishes only briefly and proportionally, always returns to cooperation with an unconditional olive branch, and absolutely refuses to defect when the stock is endangered \u2014 prioritizing collective sustainability over short-term retaliation gains, even in the final round when the pool is fragile."

description_BENEVOLENT_2 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nState tracked: number of opponent cooperators last round (`coop_prev`), current stock `S`, round index `t` (0-based), rounds `r`, capacity `K`.\n\n**Decision rules, evaluated in order each round:**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Critical stock protection:** If S < 0.2 \u00d7 K, cooperate unconditionally \u2014 regardless of history \u2014 to give the pool the best chance to regenerate. Punishing at low stock only accelerates collapse and hurts everyone.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r \u2212 1):** Cooperate if a majority of opponents cooperated last round (coop_prev \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309); otherwise defect. No sustainability rationale remains, but cooperation is still extended to a cooperative group rather than exploiting it.\n\n4. **Main rule (generous majority reciprocation):** Let f = coop_prev / (n\u22121), the fraction of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: Cooperate.\n   - If f < 0.5: Defect this round only, then apply generosity: with the next round treated as a fresh start, cooperate again (i.e., punishment lasts exactly one round; after a punishing round, cooperate regardless of coop_prev, unless coop_prev = 0).\n   - If coop_prev = 0 (universal defection): defect until at least one opponent cooperates in a round, but still override with rule 2 if stock is critical, and additionally play a single \"olive branch\" cooperation every 3rd consecutive defection round to test whether opponents will re-establish cooperation.\n\n5. **Recovery reward:** If stock increased since the previous round (S_t > S_{t\u22121}), cooperate regardless of coop_prev \u2014 rising stock indicates collective restraint, and this rule locks in recovery.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif S < 0.2*K: play C\nelif t == r-1: play C if coop_prev >= ceil((n-1)/2) else D\nelif S > S_prev: play C\nelif coop_prev == 0:\n    play C if (consecutive_D_rounds % 3 == 2) else D\nelif coop_prev/(n-1) >= 0.5: play C\nelif punished_last_round: play C   # one-round punishment only\nelse: play D; punished_last_round = True\n```\n\n(`punished_last_round` resets to False whenever C is played.)\n\n**Benevolence:** The strategy defaults to cooperation, never initiates defection, forgives after a single round of punishment, sacrifices short-term payoff to rescue a collapsing stock (rule 2), periodically offers olive branches even against full defection, and declines to exploit cooperators in the final round."

description_BENEVOLENT_3 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\n**Core state tracked:** each round, record the number of other players who cooperated last round, `coop_last` (out of n\u22121), and the current stock fraction `f = stock/capacity`.\n\n**Decision rules, in order of priority:**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock emergency \u2014 always cooperate:** if `f < 0.4`, play C regardless of history. Defecting on a depleted pool accelerates collapse and hurts everyone; cooperation maximizes the chance of regrowth toward the sustainable level.\n\n3. **Stock healthy and opponents mostly cooperative \u2014 cooperate:** if `coop_last \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309`, play C.\n\n4. **Majority defected last round \u2014 probabilistic reciprocation:** if `coop_last < \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309` and `f \u2265 0.4`, defect with probability `1 \u2212 coop_last/(n\u22121)`, otherwise cooperate. (Pseudocode: `if random() < coop_last/(n-1): play C else play D`.) This proportionally matches the defection level around me without escalating, and always leaves a path back to full cooperation.\n\n5. **Forgiveness override:** after any round in which I defected, if `coop_last` did not decrease compared to the round before, cooperate this round unconditionally (offer an olive branch). This prevents mutual-punishment spirals.\n\n6. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** if `coop_last \u2265 n\u22121` (everyone cooperated last round) **and** `f \u2265 0.8`, still cooperate \u2014 reward a fully cooperative group rather than exploit it. Otherwise, defect only if `f \u2265 0.4` and `coop_last < \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309`; else cooperate. Note there is no last-round grab against cooperators: benevolence over endgame exploitation.\n\n**Summary pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, stock, history):\n    f = stock / capacity\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if f < 0.4: return C                      # protect the pool\n    k = coop_last / (n - 1)\n    if t == r - 1:\n        if k == 1 and f >= 0.8: return C\n        return D if k < 0.5 else C\n    if i_defected_last and coop_did_not_drop: return C   # olive branch\n    if k >= 0.5: return C\n    return C with prob k, else D              # measured reciprocity\n```\n\n**Benevolent character:** the strategy opens with trust, cooperates whenever cooperation is at least matched by the group, never punishes harder than it was provoked, forgives quickly, sacrifices short-term gain to rescue a depleted stock, and declines to exploit fully cooperative opponents even in the final round. Defection is used only as a limited, proportional signal against sustained majority free-riding when the stock can absorb it."

description_BENEVOLENT_4 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nMaintain: `defectors_last` = number of opponents who defected in the previous round; `forgiveness_counter`.\n\n**Decision rule each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Play C.\n\n2. **Stock-preservation override:** If S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, play C unconditionally (regardless of history), except in the final round. Protecting the pool comes first.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If S is healthy (S \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity) and a majority of opponents cooperated last round (defectors_last \u2264 (n\u22121)/2), play C anyway \u2014 do not exploit the endgame against cooperators. Otherwise play D.\n\n4. **Main rule (rounds 1 to r\u22122):**\n   - Let f = defectors_last / (n\u22121), the fraction of opponents that defected.\n   - If f \u2264 1/3: play C.\n   - If f > 1/3: play D with probability f, C otherwise (proportional, measured retaliation), **but** with generosity \u2014 with probability 0.1, override any D and play C to allow recovery from mutual-punishment spirals.\n   - Never punish for more than 2 consecutive rounds: if I have played D in the last 2 rounds, play C this round (reset forgiveness_counter).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif S < 0.2*capacity and t < r-1: return C\nif t == r-1:\n    return C if (S >= 0.5*capacity and defectors_last <= (n-1)/2) else D\nf = defectors_last / (n-1)\nif f <= 1/3: return C\nif my last two moves were D: return C\nif random() < 0.1: return C          # generosity\nreturn D if random() < f else C\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- If stock hits exactly 0, play C every remaining round (all actions yield 0; cooperation is costless and signals goodwill in case rounding/growth revives the stock).\n- If n = 2, f is 0 or 1: cooperate after opponent's C; after opponent's D, defect once then return to C (tit-for-tat with immediate forgiveness).\n- If stock recovers above 0.6 \u00d7 capacity after having been low, reset all grudges: play C and clear the punishment state.\n\n**Benevolent alignment:** The strategy leads with cooperation, defaults to cooperation whenever a majority cooperates, sacrifices short-term gain to protect a depleted stock (even absorbing exploitation when S is low), retaliates only proportionally and briefly, forgives randomly and after recovery, and refuses end-game exploitation of cooperative partners. Punishment exists solely to deter stock destruction, never to maximize relative advantage."

description_BENEVOLENT_5 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\n**Core idea:** Cooperate by default, punish widespread defection briefly and proportionally, forgive quickly, and always cooperate when the stock is fragile so the pool can recover.\n\n**Notation:** Let d_t = number of opponents who defected in round t (out of n\u22121). Maintain a punishment counter P (initially 0). Let S = current stock.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock-protection override:** If S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, Cooperate \u2014 regardless of history. A depleted pool needs healing, and defecting on low stock yields little anyway.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Cooperate if S \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity and fewer than half of opponents defected last round (d_{t\u22121} < (n\u22121)/2); otherwise Defect. (Never defect on a fragile stock, even at the end.)\n\n4. **Punishment state:** If P > 0: Defect, decrement P by 1. (But rule 2 overrides: if stock is fragile, cooperate and still decrement P.)\n\n5. **Trigger check:** After observing round t\u22121:\n   - If d_{t\u22121} > (n\u22121)/2 (majority defected): set P = 1 (single-round proportional response), so Defect this round unless the stock override applies.\n   - If d_{t\u22121} \u2265 1 but \u2264 (n\u22121)/2: with generosity, Cooperate anyway \u2014 but if this is the third consecutive round with any defection, set P = 1.\n   - If d_{t\u22121} = 0: Cooperate and reset the consecutive-defection counter.\n\n6. **Default:** Cooperate.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ninit: P = 0, streak = 0\n\neach round t with stock S:\n    if t == 0: play C; continue\n    fragile = S < 0.4 * capacity\n    if fragile: play C; P = max(P-1, 0); continue\n    if t == r-1:\n        play C if d_prev < (n-1)/2 else D; continue\n    if P > 0: play D; P -= 1; continue\n    if d_prev > (n-1)/2: P stays 0 but play D this round\n    elif d_prev >= 1:\n        streak += 1\n        if streak >= 3: play D; streak = 0\n        else: play C\n    else:\n        streak = 0; play C\n```\n\n**Benevolence properties:**\n- Against all-cooperators, it never defects \u2014 full mutual sustainability, stock stays at capacity.\n- Punishments last exactly one round; forgiveness is immediate, preventing echo wars of retaliation.\n- Tolerates minority defection (up to two consecutive rounds) without retaliating, absorbing noise and exploratory defections.\n- Prioritizes the common resource over personal punishment: it never defects when the stock is below 40% of capacity, sacrificing short-term retaliation to restore the pool for everyone.\n- End-game defection occurs only when the environment is already uncooperative and the stock can bear it."

description_BENEVOLENT_6 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\n**Core idea:** Cooperate by default, mirror the group's cooperation level with generosity, and always cooperate when the stock is endangered \u2014 sustaining the pool matters more than punishing.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock-protection override:** If S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, Cooperate unconditionally. A depleted pool hurts everyone; never accelerate collapse regardless of history.\n\n3. **Recovery grace:** If the stock increased last round (S_t > S_{t-1}), lean cooperative: Cooperate unless fewer than \u2308(n\u22121)/3\u2309 opponents cooperated last round.\n\n4. **Otherwise (main rule):** Let c = number of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If c \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309: Cooperate.\n   - Else: Defect with probability 0.8, Cooperate with probability 0.2 (generous forgiveness to allow re-coordination on cooperation).\n\n5. **Return to cooperation:** After any round in which all n\u22121 opponents cooperated, cooperate for the next two rounds regardless of noise, to help stabilize a cooperative equilibrium.\n\n6. **Last round:** Apply the same rules \u2014 no end-game defection. If the group has been mostly cooperative (average opponent cooperation over the game \u2265 50%), cooperate in round r\u22121; otherwise apply rule 4. Rule 2 still overrides: cooperate if S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity even in the final round.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif S < 0.4 * capacity: return C\nif last two rounds had full opponent cooperation streak: return C\nc = opponents_cooperated_last_round\nif stock_increased_last_round and c >= ceil((n-1)/3): return C\nif c >= ceil((n-1)/2): return C\nif t == r-1 and avg_opponent_coop_rate >= 0.5: return C\nreturn D with prob 0.8, else C\n```\n\n**Benevolence:** The strategy opens cooperatively, never defects first, forgives probabilistically, refuses to exploit the final round against a cooperative group, and \u2014 most importantly \u2014 sacrifices its own short-term gain to protect a threatened stock, ensuring the resource survives for all players."

description_BENEVOLENT_7 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nMaintain: `coop_rate` = fraction of opponents who cooperated last round; `forgive_counter`.\n\n**Decision rule each round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Play C.\n\n2. **Critical stock protection:** If `stock < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity`, play C unconditionally (help the pool recover, regardless of others' behavior).\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If the majority of opponents cooperated last round (`coop_rate \u2265 0.5`) or stock < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, play C; otherwise play D. (Even at the end, don't exploit a cooperative group.)\n\n4. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22122):**\n   - Let `k` = number of opponents who cooperated last round, out of n\u22121.\n   - If `k \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309`: play C (reciprocate a mostly-cooperative group).\n   - If `k < \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309`:\n     - With generous forgiveness: every third consecutive round of majority-defection (`forgive_counter % 3 == 2`), play C anyway to offer an olive branch and reset `forgive_counter` to 0; increment it otherwise.\n     - Additionally, if `stock \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity` (pool is healthy despite defection, meaning few defectors overall or recovery is strong), play C instead of D.\n     - Otherwise play D \u2014 but only as a bounded, proportionate response; never defect more than 2 consecutive rounds before testing cooperation again.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return C\nif stock < 0.2 * capacity: return C\nk = cooperators_among_opponents_last_round\n\nif t == r - 1:\n    return C if (k >= (n-1)/2 or stock < 0.5*capacity) else D\n\nif k >= ceil((n-1)/2):\n    forgive_counter = 0\n    return C\nelse:\n    if stock >= 0.9 * capacity:\n        return C\n    forgive_counter += 1\n    if forgive_counter >= 3:\n        forgive_counter = 0\n        return C   # olive branch\n    return D\n```\n\n**Benevolent character:** Starts cooperatively, always cooperates when the resource is endangered (sacrificing short-term gain to preserve the commons for everyone), reciprocates cooperation immediately, forgives defection regularly and unconditionally after at most two retaliatory rounds, and refuses to exploit cooperative opponents even in the final round."

description_BENEVOLENT_8 = "**Strategy: Generous Stock-Guardian Tit-for-Tat**\n\nMaintain a variable `forgiving = true` and track last round's observed cooperators among opponents, `k` (out of n\u22121).\n\n**Decision rule each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate.\n\n2. **Stock-protection override (highest priority):** If S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, Cooperate unconditionally. A depleted pool must be allowed to regrow; punishing with D at low stock destroys everyone's future payoffs, including mine.\n\n3. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** If S \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity and last round's cooperation fraction k/(n\u22121) < 1/2, Defect (no future to protect and majority already defecting). Otherwise Cooperate \u2014 reward a cooperative group even at the end.\n\n4. **Main rule (rounds 1 \u2026 r\u22122):** Let f = k/(n\u22121), the fraction of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If f \u2265 1/2: Cooperate.\n   - If f < 1/2: Defect this round *unless* forgiveness triggers.\n   - **Generous forgiveness:** After defecting due to low f, cooperate for one \"olive branch\" round every 3rd punishing round (i.e., if the number of consecutive punishment rounds \u2261 0 mod 3, play C instead of D). This probes whether opponents have returned to cooperation and avoids permanent mutual-defection lock-in.\n\n5. **Recovery reward:** If f improved by at least 1/(n\u22121) compared to two rounds ago (any opponent switched back to C), immediately return to Cooperate, resetting the punishment counter.\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif S < 0.4*capacity: play C\nelif t == r-1: play D if f < 0.5 else C\nelif f >= 0.5 or f_improved or punish_count % 3 == 0: play C; reset punish_count if f>=0.5\nelse: play D; punish_count += 1\n```\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- n = 2: f is 0 or 1; rule reduces to generous tit-for-tat with the stock override.\n- Stock = 0: actions are payoff-irrelevant; play C (costless goodwill, and consistent bookkeeping).\n- Very short games (r = 2): Round 0 cooperate; round 1 apply the last-round rule.\n\n**Benevolent alignment:** The strategy opens with cooperation, cooperates whenever even half the group does, sacrifices short-term punishment payoffs to let a damaged stock recover (protecting the commons benefits everyone), regularly extends forgiveness to rebuild cooperation, and defects in the final round only against a majority-defecting group \u2014 never exploiting cooperative opponents."

description_SELFISH_1 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Extraction with Endgame Grab\"**\n\nState tracked: last round's number of cooperating opponents `c_prev` (of n\u22121), current stock S, round index t (0-based), a defection-count `strikes` (consecutive rounds where opponents' cooperation fell below threshold).\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or if `c_prev` < n\u22121; otherwise (n = 2 with a fully cooperative opponent) cooperate to keep stock high for the final grab.\n\n3. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Stock is at capacity; probe for cooperative opponents.\n\n4. **Middle rounds (0 < t < r\u22122):**\n   - Compute cooperation rate \u03c1 = c_prev / (n\u22121).\n   - **Stock collapse check:** if S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, the pool is nearly dead \u2014 the future is worth little, so **Defect** (harvest the remains).\n   - **Reciprocity threshold:** Cooperate iff \u03c1 \u2265 1/2 AND `strikes` < 2.\n     - Update strikes: if \u03c1 < 1/2, strikes += 1; else strikes = 0.\n   - **Forgiveness/regrowth probe:** if currently in defection mode (strikes \u2265 2) but stock has recovered above 0.6 \u00d7 capacity AND last round's \u03c1 \u2265 1/2, reset strikes to 0 and Cooperate (re-establish the profitable sustainable regime).\n   - **Exploitation window:** if \u03c1 = 1 (all opponents cooperated last round) and S \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity and t < r\u22123, defect once every 4th such opportunity (i.e., defect when a counter of full-cooperation rounds \u2261 3 mod 4). All-C keeps stock at capacity, so a rare solo defection is nearly free extra payoff; keeping frequency low avoids triggering opponents' punishment. Skip this if it triggered opponent defection last time it was used (track a flag: if the exploitation defect was followed by \u03c1 dropping below 1, disable further exploitation defects permanently).\n\n**Rationale-in-mechanics (embedded in rules):** cooperation is only ever instrumental \u2014 maintain stock when opponents sustain it (higher absolute payoffs from a full pool), free-ride occasionally when safe, abandon restraint immediately when the pool is doomed or the horizon ends, and never cooperate in the final round.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2: return C if (n == 2 and c_prev == 1) else D\nif t == 0: exploit_enabled = True; strikes = 0; fullC_count = 0; return C\n\nrho = c_prev / (n-1)\nif S < 0.2 * capacity: return D\n\nif rho < 0.5: strikes += 1\nelse:\n    if strikes >= 2 and S >= 0.6 * capacity: strikes = 0   # forgive on recovery\n    elif strikes < 2: strikes = 0\n\nif strikes >= 2: return D\n\nif rho == 1 and S >= 0.9*capacity and t < r-3 and exploit_enabled:\n    fullC_count += 1\n    if fullC_count % 4 == 0:\n        last_was_exploit = True\n        return D\nif last_was_exploit and rho < 1: exploit_enabled = False\nlast_was_exploit = False\nreturn C\n```"

description_SELFISH_2 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Extraction with Generous Trigger\"**\n\nTrack after each round t: d_t = number of defectors among opponents (n\u22121 minus observed cooperators), and stock S_t.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or if the opponent defected in either of the last two rounds; otherwise cooperate only if stock is at full capacity and the opponent has been fully cooperative throughout (in 2-player games, preserving stock for one more round can pay).\n\n3. **Endgame lead-in:** If stock is low (S_t < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity) and t \u2265 r\u22123, defect \u2014 regrowth cannot recover enough value to justify restraint.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Signal willingness to sustain the pool.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123), use a tolerance rule based on recent opponent behavior:**\n   - Compute f = fraction of opponents who defected last round, i.e., d_{t\u22121}/(n\u22121).\n   - **Cooperate if f \u2264 1/n** (tolerate roughly one defector per n players \u2014 a small leak is worth sustaining the pool for my own future harvests).\n   - **Defect if f > 1/2** \u2014 the pool is being drained regardless; grab my share before it's gone.\n   - **Intermediate zone (1/n < f \u2264 1/2):** cooperate with probability proportional to remaining rounds and stock health: cooperate iff S_t \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity AND the average defection fraction over the last min(3, t) rounds is \u2264 1/3. Otherwise defect.\n\n6. **Stock-collapse override:** If S_t < 0.25 \u00d7 capacity at any round, defect. At that level, per-round cooperative income is tiny; extract while anything remains. Exception: if t \u2264 r\u22124 and every opponent cooperated last round, cooperate once to let the stock regrow (regrowth is fastest at mid-stock, and I capture that recovery).\n\n7. **Recovery re-entry:** After defecting due to rules 5\u20136, return to cooperation if the last round showed all opponents cooperating AND S_t \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity AND t < r\u22123. This exploits any group willing to rebuild the pool \u2014 I profit more from a sustained pool than from finishing off a depleted one.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, r, n, S, capacity, history):\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if t >= r-2:\n        if n == 2 and S == capacity and all_opponent_moves_C(history): return C\n        return D\n    if t >= r-3 and S < 0.4*capacity: return D\n    f = defectors_last_round / (n-1)\n    if S < 0.25*capacity:\n        return C if (f == 0 and t <= r-4) else D\n    if f <= 1/n: return C\n    if f > 0.5: return D\n    recent = avg_defection_fraction(last 3 rounds)\n    return C if (S >= 0.6*capacity and recent <= 1/3) else D\n```\n\nThe core logic is purely self-interested: cooperation is investment \u2014 I forgo stock/(2n) now only when regrowth and likely opponent cooperation make my future harvests worth more. Whenever the pool is doomed, the horizon is short, or opponents are extracting heavily, I take the maximum share immediately."

description_SELFISH_3 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Threshold Retaliation**\n\nMaintain a variable `defectors_last` = number of opponents who defected in the previous round, and a rolling estimate `coop_rate` = fraction of opponent-cooperation over the last 3 rounds (or all history if fewer).\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future stock to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect if** stock is high enough that depletion barely matters, i.e. if expected opponents' behavior implies stock will still exceed 0.25 \u00d7 capacity next round assuming I defect; otherwise cooperate. (Compute: S_rem = stock \u2212 stock/n \u2212 (n\u22121)\u00b7[coop_rate\u00b7stock/(2n) + (1\u2212coop_rate)\u00b7stock/n]; next_stock = min(S_rem + 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1\u2212S_rem/capacity), capacity); defect if next_stock \u2265 0.25\u00b7capacity, else cooperate.)\n\n3. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Full stock is worth preserving; probe opponents.\n\n4. **Otherwise (middle rounds):**\n   - **Collapse guard:** if stock < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity, **Cooperate** \u2014 a small pool must regrow; defecting on a tiny stock earns pennies and kills future income.\n   - **Exploitation check:** if `defectors_last` \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309, **Defect** \u2014 the pool is being drained anyway; don't subsidize defectors' harvest, grab your share.\n   - **Free-ride opportunistically:** if stock \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity AND `defectors_last` \u2264 1, defect with probability 1/r each round (at most once every few rounds) to skim surplus while the pool regenerates near capacity; otherwise cooperate.\n   - **Forgiveness:** if you defected last round in retaliation and `defectors_last` has dropped below \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309, return to **Cooperate** \u2014 restoring the productive pool serves your future payoffs.\n   - Default: **Cooperate.**\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2: return D if projected_next_stock(D) >= 0.25*capacity else C\nif t == 0: return C\nif stock < 0.2*capacity: return C\nif defectors_last >= ceil((n-1)/2): return D\nif stock >= 0.9*capacity and defectors_last <= 1 and rand() < 1/r: return D\nreturn C\n```\n\nThe cooperation here is purely instrumental: keeping stock near capacity maximizes the base from which every future harvest \u2014 including your terminal defections \u2014 is drawn. Defection is triggered exactly when the future is short, the pool is doomed by others, or a near-full pool makes skimming free."

description_SELFISH_4 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Exploitation**\n\nMaintain state: `defections_last_round` (number of opponents who played D last round), and a `punish_counter` (rounds of punishment remaining, initially 0).\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if stock after a mass-defection collapse doesn't matter much anyway \u2014 specifically, defect if S/n from this round plus expected leftover exceeds cooperating; in practice: defect unless the number of cooperators last round was \u2265 n\u22121 (i.e., near-full cooperation, where cooperating keeps stock high for the final defection round). Formally: cooperate at t = r\u22122 only if defections_last_round \u2264 1 and S \u2265 0.75 \u00d7 capacity; otherwise defect.\n\n3. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, defect (grab scraps; growth can't recover meaningful value, and consuming little of nothing sacrifices nothing).\n\n4. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate. Full stock, maximum value in sustaining the pool.\n\n5. **Punishment mode:** If punish_counter > 0, defect and decrement punish_counter.\n\n6. **Otherwise (middle rounds):** Let d = defections_last_round.\n   - If d = 0: cooperate.\n   - If 1 \u2264 d \u2264 \u2308n/4\u2309: cooperate, but only if stock is healthy (S \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity); if S < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, defect for one round (the pool is being overexploited \u2014 take your share before it's gone), then re-evaluate.\n   - If d > \u2308n/4\u2309: defect and set punish_counter = 1 (defect this round and next). This both punishes and captures value from a pool likely doomed by others.\n\n7. **Recovery override:** If in step 6 you would defect due to low stock but d = 0 last round (everyone cooperated and stock is regrowing), cooperate instead \u2014 a fully cooperative population regrows the pool, and staying cooperative keeps that equilibrium alive for your endgame harvest.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.05 * capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nif punish_counter > 0: punish_counter -= 1; return D\nif t == r-2:\n    return C if (d <= 1 and S >= 0.75*capacity) else D\nif d == 0: return C\nif d <= ceil(n/4):\n    return C if S >= 0.5*capacity else D\npunish_counter = 1\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe logic is purely selfish: cooperation is used only as an investment to keep the stock (and hence the value of future extraction, especially the guaranteed final-round defection) high. The moment opponents make sustainability unlikely, or the horizon makes the investment unrecoverable, switch to maximal extraction."

description_SELFISH_5 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Exploitation**\n\nTrack: last round's cooperator count among opponents, `coop_prev` (out of n\u22121), and current stock S.\n\n**Decision rules, round t (0-indexed, r rounds total):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if n \u2265 3 or if stock is high (S \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity); otherwise apply rule 4. (With many players, one extra defection barely changes final-round stock, so grabbing S/n now beats S/(2n).)\n\n3. **Collapse guard:** If S < 0.15\u00b7capacity, cooperate \u2014 a depleted pool pays almost nothing now, and light harvesting lets the stock regrow so future rounds are worth more (regrowth is fastest at low-to-mid stock). Exception: still defect in the last round.\n\n4. **Main rule (generous tit-for-tat on the majority):**\n   - Round 0: Cooperate (probe for a cooperative population; the cost is only S/(2n)).\n   - Otherwise, let f = coop_prev / (n\u22121):\n     - If f \u2265 0.5: Cooperate.\n     - If f < 0.5: Defect \u2014 the pool is being drained regardless, so take the larger share while it lasts.\n   - **Forgiveness:** If defecting due to f < 0.5, cooperate anyway with probability 0.1 (once per streak of defection, i.e., at most every other round) to test whether the population has returned to cooperation \u2014 unless t \u2265 r\u22123.\n\n5. **Opportunistic skim:** Even when rule 4 says cooperate, defect if S is at capacity AND all opponents cooperated last round AND the projected post-round stock with my single defection still regrows to \u2265 0.9\u00b7capacity (compute: S_rem = S \u2212 (n\u22121)\u00b7S/(2n) \u2212 S/n; S_new = min(S_rem + 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1 \u2212 S_rem/capacity), capacity)). Do this at most once every 3 rounds so as not to trigger opponents' punishment persistently.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, r, n, S, capacity, coop_prev, defect_streak, last_skim):\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2 and (n >= 3 or S >= 0.5*capacity): return D\n    if S < 0.15*capacity: return C\n    if t == 0: return C\n    f = coop_prev / (n-1)\n    if f >= 0.5:\n        if S >= capacity*0.999 and f == 1.0 and t - last_skim >= 3 \\\n           and projected_stock_after_one_defect(S) >= 0.9*capacity \\\n           and t < r-3:\n            return D  # record last_skim = t\n        return C\n    else:\n        if random() < 0.1 and t < r-3 and defect_streak >= 1:\n            return C\n        return D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout is purely self-interested: cooperation is only ever an investment in a larger future stock from which I harvest; whenever the future can't repay that investment (endgame, collapsing majority, or a free skim that regrows), I take the larger share."

description_SELFISH_6 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Extraction with Endgame Grab\"**\n\nMaintain per-round variables: t (current round, 0-indexed), r, n, stock S, and d_prev = number of defectors observed last round (n minus cooperators, including self if I defected).\n\n**Decision rules, evaluated in order:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future stock to protect.\n\n2. **Endgame trigger:** Defect if t \u2265 r \u2212 2 and S is low (S < 0.25 \u00d7 capacity), or always if t = r\u22121. Additionally, defect on round r\u22122 if d_prev \u2265 n/2 (cooperation is already collapsing; grab while there's stock).\n\n3. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, defect. The stock is nearly worthless and regrowth is negligible; squeeze out the residual value rather than subsidize others' recovery.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Probe for a cooperative population and keep the stock at capacity.\n\n5. **Forgiving proportional trigger (rounds 1 to r\u22122):**\n   - Track exponentially smoothed defection rate: D\u0302 \u2190 0.6 \u00d7 (d_prev / n) + 0.4 \u00d7 D\u0302 (initialize D\u0302 = 0).\n   - **Defect if D\u0302 > 0.5** \u2014 majority-defecting environments make conservation a pure gift; extract fast before the pool dies.\n   - **Defect if D\u0302 > 0.25 AND S < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity** \u2014 the pool is being drained and not enough players are sustaining it; my cooperation can't save it, so take my share.\n   - **Otherwise cooperate** \u2014 with mostly-cooperative opponents, a healthy stock compounds: one round of C at high stock beats D followed by permanently lower stock (all-C keeps S at capacity, yielding S/(2n) forever; defecting depresses S for all remaining rounds).\n\n6. **Recovery clause:** If currently in a defecting mode via rule 5 but d_prev \u2264 1 and S \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity, return to cooperation next round \u2014 opponents have restored order and the long-run cooperative payoff again exceeds grabbing.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.05*cap: return D\nif t == r-2 and (S < 0.25*cap or d_prev >= n/2): return D\nif t == 0: D_hat = 0; return C\nD_hat = 0.6*(d_prev/n) + 0.4*D_hat\nif d_prev <= 1 and S >= 0.6*cap: return C\nif D_hat > 0.5: return D\nif D_hat > 0.25 and S < 0.5*cap: return D\nreturn C\n```\n\n**Selfish logic embedded throughout:** cooperation is used purely instrumentally \u2014 only when it maintains a stock whose future extraction value to *me* exceeds the immediate defection bonus. Whenever the pool is doomed, opponents are exploitative, or no future rounds remain, defect and take the maximum share."

description_SELFISH_7 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Extraction with Endgame Grab\"**\n\nState tracked: last round's number of cooperating opponents `coop_prev`, current stock `S`, round index `t` (0-based), rounds remaining `R = r - t`.\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Last round (R = 1): Defect.** No future stock to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (R = 2): Defect** if `coop_prev < n - 1` (some opponents already defecting, so preservation gains are shared away) \u2014 otherwise defect anyway unless stock regrowth is strongly convex: in practice, **always defect when R = 2** if `S \u2265 capacity/2`; cooperate only if `S < capacity/2` and `coop_prev \u2265 n - 1` (protect a fragile stock one more round to harvest it in the finale).\n\n3. **Stock-collapse guard:** If `S < 0.2 \u00d7 capacity` and `R \u2265 3`, **cooperate** regardless of history \u2014 a depleted pool destroys all future payoffs; letting it recover benefits me more than a large share of almost nothing.\n\n4. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123), use a generous majority-conditional rule:**\n   - Round 0: **Cooperate** (probe; sustaining full stock is highly profitable, and cooperation costs only S/(2n) now).\n   - Round t \u2265 1: Cooperate iff `coop_prev \u2265 ceil((n-1)/2)` (at least half of opponents cooperated last round). If fewer than half cooperated, **defect** \u2014 the pool is being drained anyway, so extract while stock remains.\n   - **Forgiveness/re-probe:** If I have defected for 3 consecutive rounds and `S \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity` (stock is holding up, suggesting opponents resumed cooperating), cooperate once to re-test.\n\n5. **Opportunistic free-riding:** If `coop_prev = n - 1` (all opponents cooperated) and `S \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity`, defect with probability 1/r each round (rare skim): the stock regenerates fully from n\u22121 cooperators plus one defector when near capacity, so occasional defection is nearly free \u2014 but keep frequency low to avoid triggering opponents' punishment rules.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, coop_prev, n, r, capacity, my_history):\n    R = r - t\n    if R == 1: return D\n    if R == 2:\n        if S < capacity/2 and coop_prev >= n-1: return C\n        return D\n    if S < 0.2*capacity: return C\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if coop_prev >= ceil((n-1)/2):\n        if coop_prev == n-1 and S >= 0.9*capacity and rand() < 1/r:\n            return D\n        return C\n    # opponents mostly defecting\n    if last_3(my_history) == [D,D,D] and S >= 0.6*capacity:\n        return C   # re-probe\n    return D\n```\n\nEvery cooperation choice is an investment in my own future harvest, never altruism: I cooperate only when the stock's regrowth (which I later extract, culminating in guaranteed defection in the final rounds) exceeds the immediate S/(2n) sacrificed, and I defect the moment opponents' behavior makes conservation unprofitable for me."

description_SELFISH_8 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Contingent Punishment (SECP)**\n\nState tracked: for each past round, number of other cooperators `c_t` (out of n\u22121), stock trajectory.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Endgame defection:** If t = r\u22121 (last round), play D. If t = r\u22122 and n \u2264 4, also play D (anticipate cascade only when my share is large).\n\n2. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, play D \u2014 the resource is effectively gone; grab what remains, growth can't save it.\n\n3. **First round:** Play C (probe for a cooperative population while keeping stock near capacity).\n\n4. **Otherwise, condition on last round's observed cooperation** `c` (other cooperators out of n\u22121):\n   - Let `coop_frac = c / (n\u22121)`.\n   - **If coop_frac \u2265 0.5:** Play C \u2014 the pool regenerates well and the stream of future S/(2n) payoffs at high stock beats one-shot gains.\n   - **If coop_frac < 0.5:** Play D for one round (punish / avoid being the sucker), **but** apply a recovery override: if stock has fallen below 0.4 \u00d7 capacity AND at least 3 rounds remain, play C instead. Reason: with a depleted pool, my future selfish earnings depend on stock regrowth; a small consumption sacrifice now multiplies my later per-round take.\n\n5. **Forgiveness/re-probe:** If I have defected for 2 consecutive rounds due to rule 4, play C once (regardless of `coop_frac`) provided at least 3 rounds remain and S \u2265 0.2 \u00d7 capacity. This tests whether the population has returned to cooperation; if the probe fails (coop_frac still < 0.5 next round), resume rule 4.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2 and n <= 4: return D\n    if S < 0.05*capacity: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    c = history[-1].other_cooperators\n    if c/(n-1) >= 0.5:\n        return C\n    # low cooperation\n    if S < 0.4*capacity and (r-t) >= 3:\n        return C            # invest in regrowth for my own future harvest\n    if last_two_my_moves == [D, D] and (r-t) >= 3 and S >= 0.2*capacity:\n        return C            # probe\n    return D\n```\n\nAll cooperation here is instrumental: I cooperate only when doing so raises my own remaining-game harvest via stock regeneration or by sustaining a cooperative regime I exploit at the end; otherwise I defect."

description_SELFINTERESTED_1 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain two tracked quantities: last round's number of cooperating opponents `c_prev` (out of n\u22121), and a defection-streak counter for punishment.\n\n**Decision rules, round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or if `c_prev < n\u22121`; otherwise (n = 2 and opponent cooperated last round) Cooperate \u2014 one final round of mutual sustainability is worth more only in tiny games; in practice defect here too unless n = 2 and full cooperation has held every round so far.\n\n3. **Stock nearly dead (S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity): Defect.** The pool cannot meaningfully recover; extract remaining value.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Probe for a cooperative population; the cost of one C at full stock is small relative to preserving capacity.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123):** Let `f = c_prev / (n\u22121)` be the observed opponent cooperation rate last round.\n   - **Cooperate if f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121)** (i.e., at most one opponent defected). Full or near-full cooperation keeps stock at capacity, and sustaining that yields far more than one-shot gains.\n   - **Cooperate with forgiveness:** if f \u2265 0.5 but at most one defection occurred in each of the last two rounds, still cooperate \u2014 tolerate noise.\n   - **Defect if f < 0.5**: the stock is being destroyed regardless; take the larger share while it lasts.\n   - **Intermediate zone (0.5 \u2264 f < (n\u22122)/(n\u22121), persistent):** Defect for exactly one round (punish), then return to Cooperate the following round regardless of observation (olive branch). If after two such punish\u2013forgive cycles cooperation rate hasn't recovered to f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121), switch to permanent Defect for the remainder of the game.\n\n6. **Recovery override:** if currently in permanent-defect mode but stock has regrown above 0.6 \u00d7 capacity AND all opponents cooperated last round, exit permanent-defect mode and cooperate (opponents may have coordinated back to sustainability worth rejoining).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nstate: mode \u2208 {NORMAL, PUNISH, GRIM}, cycles_used = 0\n\ndecide(t, S, history):\n  if t == r-1: return D\n  if S < 0.05*capacity: return D\n  if t == 0: return C\n  if t == r-2 and not (n == 2 and all rounds fully cooperative): return D\n\n  f = c_prev / (n-1)\n  if mode == GRIM:\n      if S > 0.6*capacity and f == 1: mode = NORMAL; return C\n      return D\n  if mode == PUNISH:\n      mode = NORMAL   # olive branch\n      return C\n  if f >= (n-2)/(n-1): return C\n  if f < 0.5: mode = GRIM; return D\n  # intermediate defection level\n  cycles_used += 1\n  if cycles_used > 2: mode = GRIM; return D\n  mode = PUNISH; return D\n```\n\nAll thresholds are computed from parameters; no communication or identity tracking is needed."

description_SELFINTERESTED_2 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nTrack after each round: `coop_count` = number of opponents who cooperated last round, and current `stock`.\n\n**Decision rules (round t, 0-indexed, r rounds total):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or stock is low (< capacity/2); otherwise apply rule 5 (with n=2 mutual cooperation in r\u22122 may preserve stock worth more than one defection gain \u2014 cooperate only if opponent cooperated last round).\n\n3. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Stock is at capacity; probe for cooperators.\n\n4. **Collapse check:** If stock < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, **Defect** \u2014 the pool is effectively dead, so extract whatever remains; regrowth cannot repay restraint.\n\n5. **Middle rounds (0 < t < r\u22122):** Use a forgiving threshold rule based on last round's opponent cooperation fraction f = coop_count/(n\u22121):\n   - If f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121) \u2014 i.e., at most one opponent defected \u2014 **Cooperate.**\n   - If f is below that threshold, **Defect** for one round (punishment), then return to **Cooperate** once (olive branch). Concretely, maintain a flag:\n     ```\n     if punished_last_round:\n         play C   # test whether group returned to cooperation\n     elif f >= (n-2)/(n-1):\n         play C\n     else:\n         play D; punished_last_round = True\n     ```\n     Reset `punished_last_round = False` whenever you play C.\n   - **Persistent-defection lockout:** If in the last 3 observed rounds the majority of opponents defected each time (f < 0.5 all three rounds), switch permanently to **Defect** \u2014 the pool will collapse regardless, so grab the largest share while stock lasts.\n\n6. **Stock-recovery override:** If stock \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity and you are currently in punishment/lockout, but opponents' last-round f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121), cancel lockout and resume cooperation \u2014 the resource recovered and mutual restraint is again profitable.\n\n**Rationale encoded in behavior (self-interest):** cooperation is only a means to keep the stock high so future rounds pay more per round; whenever future value is gone (endgame, collapsed stock, hopelessly defecting group), take the D payoff which strictly dominates in the single round."

description_SELFINTERESTED_3 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain a defection count and track last round's cooperation rate among opponents.\n\n**Decision rules each round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if n \u2265 3 or if opponents' cooperation rate last round was below 0.5; otherwise cooperate (in n=2, mutual cooperation at t=r\u22122 preserves stock worth capturing at t=r\u22121).\n\n3. **Stock nearly dead (S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity):** Defect. The pool cannot meaningfully recover; grab what remains.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate. Signal willingness to sustain the pool and probe opponent types.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 \u2264 t < r\u22122), use a forgiving threshold rule:**\n   - Let `coop_frac` = fraction of opponents who cooperated in the previous round.\n   - Compute the **sustainability threshold**: cooperation is only worth it if enough others cooperate to keep stock regenerating. Defection by k players leaves S_remaining = S(1 \u2212 (n+k)/(2n)); stock is self-sustaining while total consumption \u2264 S/2, i.e., while the number of defectors k \u2264 0 strictly, but the logistic growth tolerates roughly k \u2264 n/2 before decline dominates near capacity.\n   - **Cooperate if** coop_frac \u2265 0.5 **AND** S \u2265 0.3 \u00d7 capacity.\n   - **Defect if** coop_frac < 0.5 (the pool is collapsing anyway \u2014 extract value before others do).\n   - **If S < 0.3 \u00d7 capacity but coop_frac \u2265 0.75:** cooperate anyway to allow recovery (a healthy stock next round is worth more than doubling this round's small take).\n   - Otherwise defect.\n\n6. **Forgiveness / re-entry:** The rule is memory-1 on the aggregate signal, so if opponents return to majority cooperation and stock is above threshold, resume cooperating automatically \u2014 no permanent grim trigger.\n\n7. **Exploitation probe:** If opponents have cooperated unanimously (coop_frac = 1) for 3 consecutive rounds and stock = capacity and t < r\u22124, defect once, then return to rule 5. A single defection against full cooperators is nearly costless (stock largely regenerates) and yields double payoff. Do not probe again until another 3 consecutive unanimous-cooperation rounds occur.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2: return C if (n == 2 and coop_frac_prev >= 0.5) else D\nif S < 0.05*capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nif probe_condition(): return D   # rule 7\nif coop_frac_prev >= 0.75: return C\nif coop_frac_prev >= 0.5 and S >= 0.3*capacity: return C\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout is self-interested: cooperation is chosen only when the discounted value of a sustained stock across remaining rounds exceeds the immediate doubling from defection, which holds precisely when others are mostly cooperating and the horizon isn't ending; in all other states, defect and harvest."

description_SELFINTERESTED_4 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain two tracked quantities each round t: current stock S_t, and d_{t-1} = number of opponents who defected last round.\n\n**Decision rules (in priority order):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or if the opponent defected in any of the last two rounds; otherwise cooperate only if S is at capacity and d_{t-1} = 0. (Default: Defect \u2014 endgame unraveling is one step deep.)\n\n3. **Collapse check: Defect** if the stock is effectively dead or dying \u2014 specifically if S_t < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, or if cooperating cannot plausibly keep the pool productive: compute projected next-round stock assuming all others repeat last round's action profile and I cooperate; if that projection < 0.25 \u00d7 capacity, defect (grab value before it vanishes).\n\n4. **Generous tit-for-tat on the majority:**\n   - Round 0: **Cooperate** (probe for a cooperative population; stock is full, cost of one C is small relative to sustained yields).\n   - Otherwise, cooperate iff the fraction of opponents defecting last round f = d_{t-1}/(n\u22121) \u2264 threshold \u03b8, where \u03b8 = 1/3 (i.e., tolerate a minority of defectors, since with few defectors the stock still regenerates and cooperation keeps my future base high).\n   - If f > \u03b8: **Defect** (punish/match the crowd \u2014 being a lone cooperator among defectors just subsidizes them).\n\n5. **Forgiveness / re-probe:** If currently in the defect regime (rule 4 triggered) but the stock has recovered to \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity AND d_{t-1}/(n\u22121) \u2264 \u03b8, return to cooperation. Additionally, if I have defected for 3 consecutive rounds due to rule 4 and stock \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity and at least \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309 opponents cooperated last round, play one exploratory C to test whether cooperation can be re-established (only if at least 4 rounds remain).\n\n6. **No re-probing near the end:** Never play the exploratory C of rule 5 when fewer than 4 rounds remain; in the final 4 rounds, apply rules 1\u20134 strictly with \u03b8 tightened to \u03b8 = 0.25 (less forgiving as future value shrinks).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nchoose_action(t, S, history):\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2: return D unless (n == 2 and S == capacity and no defection observed in last 2 rounds)\n    if S < 0.1*capacity: return D\n    if projected_stock_if_I_cooperate(S, last_profile) < 0.25*capacity: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    f = defectors_last_round / (n-1)\n    theta = 0.25 if (r - t) <= 4 else 1/3\n    if f <= theta: return C\n    # punishment regime\n    if S >= 0.6*capacity and f <= theta: return C   # (covered above)\n    if consecutive_D_by_me >= 3 and S >= 0.5*capacity\n       and cooperators_last_round >= ceil((n-1)/2) and (r - t) >= 4:\n        return C   # probe\n    return D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout: cooperate exactly when the discounted value of preserving a productive stock \u2014 given observed opponent cooperativeness and remaining rounds \u2014 exceeds the immediate doubling of harvest from defection; defect whenever the pool is doomed, the population is predominantly extractive, or the horizon is too short for restraint to pay back."

description_SELFINTERESTED_5 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain two tracked quantities each round t: current stock S_t, and d_{t-1} = number of opponents who defected last round.\n\n**Decision rules (in priority order):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if n \u2265 3 or if d_{t-1} \u2265 1; otherwise cooperate only if the opponent has cooperated every round so far (with n=2, one more round of mutual cooperation can be worth preserving stock \u2014 but defect anyway if stock < capacity/2).\n\n3. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate.\n\n4. **Stock-collapse check:** If S_t < 0.15 \u00d7 capacity, defect \u2014 the pool is effectively ruined; extract remaining value, since cooperation yields negligible payoff and recovery is unlikely with observed opponents.\n\n5. **Forgiving majority-based reciprocity (all other rounds):**\n   - Let f = fraction of opponents who cooperated last round = (n\u22121\u2212d_{t-1})/(n\u22121).\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: **Cooperate.**\n   - If f < 0.5: **Defect this round**, but return to cooperation next round if the observed cooperation fraction rises back to \u2265 0.5 (i.e., punish one round at a time, re-evaluating each round).\n   - **Persistent-defection lockout:** if f < 0.5 for 3 consecutive rounds, switch to permanent defection for the rest of the game \u2014 the population is exploitative and cooperation is a pure loss.\n\n6. **Stock-recovery override:** If not in lockout and S_t < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity but \u2265 0.15 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate regardless of f for one round *only if* f \u2265 0.3 (some cooperators exist worth rebuilding with); otherwise defect. Rebuilding the stock multiplies all future payoffs, so tolerate mild exploitation when the pool is damaged and partially cooperative opponents remain.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: D\nelif t == r-2:\n    if n == 2 and all rounds so far fully cooperative and S >= 0.5*capacity: C\n    else: D\nelif t == 0: C\nelif S < 0.15*capacity: D\nelif lockout (f < 0.5 for last 3 rounds): D\nelif S < 0.4*capacity:\n    C if f >= 0.3 else D\nelse:\n    C if f >= 0.5 else D\n```\n\nThis extracts full cooperation gains with cooperative populations, punishes and abandons exploitative ones quickly, invests in stock recovery only when profitable, and captures guaranteed endgame defection rents."

description_SELFINTERESTED_6 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain two internal quantities each round t with stock S:\n- d_prev = number of defectors observed last round (defined from t \u2265 1)\n- coop_rate = fraction of opponent cooperations over all past rounds\n\n**Decision rules (in order):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future stock to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or coop_rate < 0.8; otherwise cooperate (only with a single, highly cooperative opponent is preserving one more round of stock worth it).\n\n3. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, defect \u2014 payoffs are negligible and the stock rarely recovers meaningfully; grab what remains.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Stock is at capacity; signal sustainability and test the field.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123):**\n   - Compute the tolerance threshold: T = max(1, floor(n/3)).\n   - **Forgiving trigger:** If d_prev \u2264 T, cooperate.\n   - If d_prev > T, defect this round (punish/exploit a collapsing commons).\n   - **Recovery probe:** If you have defected for 2 consecutive rounds under this rule and stock is still \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity, play C once to test whether cooperation has resumed. If d_prev after the probe is again > T, return to defecting and probe again only after another 3 rounds, and only if stock \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity.\n   - **Hopeless-population override:** If coop_rate < 0.25 after at least 3 rounds of history, defect permanently \u2014 the pool is doomed, so extract maximum value from remaining stock.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndecide(t, S, history):\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2: return C if (n == 2 and coop_rate >= 0.8) else D\n    if S < 0.05 * capacity: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if t >= 3 and coop_rate < 0.25: return D\n    T = max(1, floor(n/3))\n    if d_prev <= T:\n        return C\n    else:\n        if consecutive_D_by_me >= 2 and S >= 0.4*capacity and rounds_since_last_probe >= 3:\n            return C   # probe\n        return D\n```\n\nThe logic: when opponents mostly cooperate, stock regenerates to capacity, and cooperating keeps that income stream alive \u2014 the discounted future value of a sustained pool exceeds a one-round defection gain except near the horizon. When defection is widespread, the stock will crash regardless of my choice, so I defect to capture double the per-round share of the dwindling resource. The threshold T tolerates minor defection (which mild growth can absorb) rather than triggering mutually destructive spirals, and probes allow re-establishing cooperation if the population is responsive \u2014 all serving my own cumulative payoff, not fairness."

description_SELFINTERESTED_7 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Defection**\n\nMaintain two tracked quantities each round t: current stock S_t, and d_{t-1} = number of opponents who defected last round.\n\n**Decision rules (in priority order):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if n \u2265 3 or if d_{t-2} \u2265 1; otherwise (n = 2 against a fully cooperative opponent) cooperate.\n\n3. **Collapse check:** If S_t < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, defect \u2014 the pool is effectively dead; extract what remains, since cooperation's forgone income won't be recouped.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate \u2014 establish a cooperative signal and preserve the stock.\n\n5. **Reciprocity threshold (middle rounds):** Cooperate iff the fraction of opponents defecting last round is small:\n   - If d_{t-1} = 0: cooperate.\n   - If d_{t-1} \u2265 ceil((n\u22121)/3): defect this round (punishment).\n   - Otherwise (a small minority defected): cooperate, but only if stock is healthy (S_t \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity); if S_t < 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, defect \u2014 the pool is being drained and continued restraint just subsidizes exploiters.\n\n6. **Forgiveness / regrowth check:** After defecting due to punishment (rule 5), return to cooperation on the next round if d_{t-1} drops back below ceil((n\u22121)/3) AND S_t \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity. This allows re-coordination on the sustainable path when opponents are responsive.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, r, n, S, capacity, d_prev):\n    if t == r - 1: return D\n    if t == r - 2:\n        return C if (n == 2 and d_prev == 0) else D\n    if S < 0.1 * capacity: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if d_prev == 0: return C\n    if d_prev >= ceil((n - 1) / 3): return D\n    return C if S >= 0.5 * capacity else D\n```\n\n**Rationale for self-interest embedded in the rules:** cooperation is only ever instrumental \u2014 it preserves stock so my future per-round takes (both C and D) remain large. The trigger threshold means I never keep paying the cooperation cost while a substantial fraction of opponents free-ride; the stock-health condition means I abandon restraint the moment the resource is doomed anyway; and the endgame defection captures the full one-shot dominant payoff when future stock no longer matters to me."

description_SELFINTERESTED_8 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Trigger with Endgame Harvest\"**\n\n**Core state tracked:** stock S_t, number of cooperators observed last round (k_{t-1}, out of n\u22121 opponents), and a running defection tolerance.\n\n**Decision rules, in priority order each round t:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future to protect.\n\n2. **Endgame cascade:** Defect in round t if r\u22121\u2212t < E, where E = 1 + (1 if n \u2264 3 else 0). I.e., defect in the final round always; with n \u2264 3 also defect in the second-to-last round (small groups unwind faster).\n\n3. **Dead-pool check:** If S_t < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, defect. The resource is effectively ruined; grab the remainder rather than subsidize others' regrowth.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Establish sustainability and probe opponents.\n\n5. **Conditional cooperation with forgiveness (rounds 1 \u2264 t < r\u2212E):**\n   - Let f = k_{t-1} / (n\u22121), the fraction of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If f \u2265 0.5: **Cooperate.**\n   - If f < 0.5: **Defect**, *unless* recovery test passes: if I defected last round and f has increased vs. two rounds ago and S_t \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, cooperate once as an olive branch (attempt to re-establish the high-stock equilibrium, which is worth more to me than one round's defection gain).\n   - Never extend more than one consecutive olive branch: if the previous olive branch was met with f < 0.5, defect until f \u2265 0.5 again.\n\n6. **Stock-guard override:** Even if rule 5 says defect, if S_t \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity and f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121) (at most one defector last round), cooperate \u2014 a lone defector at full stock is tolerable, and preserving full regeneration maximizes my remaining-round income.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif n <= 3 and t == r-2: return D\nif S < 0.1*capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nf = coop_count[t-1] / (n-1)\nif S >= 0.9*capacity and f >= (n-2)/(n-1): return C\nif f >= 0.5: return C\n# f < 0.5:\nif my_last == D and t >= 2 and coop_count[t-1] > coop_count[t-2] \\\n   and S >= 0.5*capacity and last_olive_failed == False:\n    mark olive branch; return C\nreturn D\n```\n\n**Rationale embedded in rules (self-interest):** Full-stock mutual cooperation yields S/(2n) = capacity/(2n) every round indefinitely, which dominates the one-shot defection gain followed by depleted-stock income. I therefore cooperate only when enough opponents sustain the pool to make future stock valuable; the moment the majority defects or the pool is near-dead, I extract maximally. The final-round defection captures the free terminal gain, and the forgiveness mechanism exists solely because restoring high-stock cooperation raises my own future payoffs, not out of fairness."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_1 = "**Opportunistic Free-Rider with Sustainability Guardrails**\n\nTrack: `coop_rate` = fraction of opponent C plays last round; `avg_coop` = exponentially weighted average (weight 0.7 on most recent round) of opponent cooperation rates.\n\n**Decision rule each round t with stock S:**\n\n1. **Endgame grab:** If t \u2265 r \u2212 2, play D. (Also play D in round r\u22123 if n \u2265 4 and stock is healthy, S \u2265 0.6\u00b7capacity.)\n\n2. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05\u00b7capacity, play D \u2014 nothing left worth preserving.\n\n3. **First round:** Play D. Probe how forgiving/reactive the field is while the stock is at max value.\n\n4. **Otherwise, exploit conditionally:**\n   - Compute a stock-health threshold \u03b8 = 0.5\u00b7capacity.\n   - **If avg_coop \u2265 (n\u22121.5)/(n\u22121)** (opponents nearly all cooperate) **and S \u2265 \u03b8:** play D \u2014 free-ride on cooperators; their restraint keeps the pool regenerating.\n   - **If avg_coop is high but S < \u03b8:** play C for one round to let regrowth restore the pool, then resume free-riding.\n   - **If avg_coop < 0.5** (field is exploitative): the pool is doomed regardless \u2014 play D and extract as much as possible before collapse.\n   - **Middle zone (0.5 \u2264 avg_coop < (n\u22121.5)/(n\u22121)):**\n     - If S \u2265 0.7\u00b7capacity: play D (stock buffer absorbs it).\n     - Else play C \u2014 invest in regrowth only when necessary, since each unit of stock preserved is future harvest.\n\n5. **Punishment detection:** If cooperation collapsed after my defections (coop_rate dropped by > 1/(n\u22121) in the two rounds following my D), play C for two consecutive rounds to rebuild opponent cooperation, then return to rule 4. Skip this repair entirely if fewer than 4 rounds remain \u2014 repairs can't pay back.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return D\nif t >= r-2: return D\nif t == r-3 and n >= 4 and S >= 0.6*cap: return D\nif S < 0.05*cap: return D\nif in_repair_mode and rounds_left >= 4: return C  # for 2 rounds\nif avg_coop < 0.5: return D\nif avg_coop >= (n-1.5)/(n-1):\n    return D if S >= 0.5*cap else C\nreturn D if S >= 0.7*cap else C\n```\n\nThe core logic: cooperate only as an investment when the pool needs regeneration and opponents will sustain it; defect whenever cooperators subsidize the pool, whenever the pool is doomed, and in every round where retaliation can no longer cost anything."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_2 = "**Opportunistic Free-Rider with Sustainability Guardrails**\n\nCore idea: defect whenever the stock can absorb it (because others cooperate enough or stock is healthy), cooperate only as an investment when defection would gut future earnings, and always defect when the future no longer matters.\n\nState tracked: coop_rate = fraction of opponents who cooperated last round (init 1.0, updated as exponential average: coop_rate \u2190 0.5\u00b7coop_rate + 0.5\u00b7observed).\n\nDecision rule each round t with stock S:\n\n1. **Endgame defection:** If t \u2265 r\u22122, play D. (Also D if t = r\u22123 and coop_rate < 0.5.)\n2. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05\u00b7capacity, play D \u2014 nothing worth preserving; grab the scraps.\n3. **Sustainability projection:** Estimate next-round stock assuming I defect and opponents cooperate at rate coop_rate:\n   - expected_consumption = S/n + (n\u22121)\u00b7[coop_rate\u00b7S/(2n) + (1\u2212coop_rate)\u00b7S/n]\n   - S_rem = S \u2212 expected_consumption; S_next = min(S_rem + 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1 \u2212 S_rem/capacity), capacity)\n4. **Opportunistic test:** Play **D** if S_next \u2265 0.6\u00b7capacity \u2014 the pool regenerates well despite my defection, so free-ride.\n5. Otherwise, compare projected stock if I cooperate instead (same formula with my consumption S/(2n) \u2192 S_next_C). Play **C** only if:\n   - S_next_C \u2212 S_next \u2265 0.1\u00b7capacity worth of regeneration gain, AND\n   - coop_rate \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121) \u2014 i.e., at most ~1 opponent defecting, so my restraint isn't wasted subsidizing exploiters, AND\n   - remaining rounds \u2265 3 (enough future to recoup the investment).\n6. If any condition in 5 fails, play **D** \u2014 either the pool is doomed anyway or others will bear the conservation cost; extract value now.\n\nEdge cases:\n- **Round 0:** Apply rule 4 with coop_rate = 1.0; with full stock this typically yields D \u2014 take the first-mover extraction bonus while others probe with cooperation.\n- **Stock at capacity with high coop_rate:** always D (rule 4 fires) \u2014 a fully cooperative field is exactly when free-riding is cheapest.\n- **Collapsing environment (coop_rate low, S falling for 2 consecutive rounds):** force D regardless of rule 5 \u2014 race to extract before others empty the pool.\n- **r = 2:** D both rounds.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\nif t >= r-2 or (t == r-3 and coop_rate < 0.5): return D\nif S < 0.05*capacity: return D\ncompute S_next (I defect), S_next_C (I cooperate)\nif S_next >= 0.6*capacity: return D\nif stock fell two rounds in a row and coop_rate < 0.5: return D\nif (S_next_C - S_next >= 0.1*capacity\n    and coop_rate >= (n-2)/(n-1)\n    and r - t >= 3): return C\nreturn D\n```"

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_3 = "**Opportunistic Free-Rider with Sustainability Guardrails**\n\nTrack each round: number of cooperators among opponents last round (`coop_last`), current `stock`, round index `t`.\n\n**Core decision rule (rounds 1 to r\u22122, 0-indexed t = 1 \u2026 r\u22122):**\n\n1. **Endgame:** if t \u2265 r\u22122 (last two rounds), play **D** unconditionally \u2014 no future stock to protect.\n2. **Stock health check:** compute `ratio = stock / capacity`.\n   - If `ratio \u2265 0.6` and `coop_last \u2265 ceil(n/2)`: play **D** \u2014 the pool is healthy and enough others are propping it up; exploit them.\n   - If `ratio \u2265 0.6` and `coop_last < ceil(n/2)`: play **D** with probability 0.5, else **C** \u2014 probe whether defection density is about to crash the stock while still grabbing extra when possible.\n   - If `0.3 \u2264 ratio < 0.6`: play **C** \u2014 invest in regrowth so there's something left to exploit later. Exception: if `coop_last = 0` (everyone else defected), play **D** \u2014 the pool is doomed; extract while you can.\n   - If `ratio < 0.3`: play **D** \u2014 the resource is near collapse; growth is weak regardless, so salvage maximum value now.\n3. **Recovery opportunism:** if in the previous round I cooperated and `coop_last \u2265 n\u22122` (near-universal cooperation), override rule 2's cooperation and play **D** this round \u2014 skim off a well-maintained pool, then return to the rules above next round.\n\n**First round (t = 0):** play **D**. Stock is at capacity, growth after consumption is maximal, and exploiting the initial abundance before any reputation dynamics exist is pure gain.\n\n**Second round adjustment:** if after round 0 `coop_last \u2265 n\u22121` (nearly everyone else cooperated), play **C** in round 1 once to let the stock recover toward capacity, then resume the core rules \u2014 this rebuilds the exploitable base.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, r, n, stock, capacity, coop_last, my_prev):\n    if t == 0: return D\n    if t >= r - 2: return D\n    if t == 1 and coop_last >= n - 1: return C\n    ratio = stock / capacity\n    if my_prev == C and coop_last >= n - 2: return D   # skim\n    if ratio >= 0.6:\n        if coop_last >= ceil(n/2): return D\n        return D if rand() < 0.5 else C\n    if ratio >= 0.3:\n        return D if coop_last == 0 else C\n    return D\n```\n\nThe strategy defects whenever others' cooperation or a healthy stock makes defection cheap, cooperates only as a minimal maintenance investment to keep the pool exploitable, and abandons all restraint the moment the resource is collapsing or the game is ending."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_4 = "**Strategy: \"Exploitative Sustainer\"**\n\nCore idea: cooperate only as much as needed to keep the stock (and thus future payoffs) alive; defect whenever the marginal defection is unlikely to crash the pool or whenever the future no longer matters.\n\nNotation: t = current round (0-indexed), S = current stock, coop_prev = number of opponents who cooperated last round (out of n\u22121).\n\nDecision rules, evaluated in order each round:\n\n1. **Endgame grab:** If t \u2265 r \u2212 2 (last two rounds), play D. Future is too short to be worth protecting. (If r = 2, this means always D.)\n\n2. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, play D \u2014 the resource is effectively gone; extract residual value.\n\n3. **Simulate a free ride:** Estimate this round's cooperators among opponents as k\u0302 = coop_prev (for t = 0, assume k\u0302 = \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309). Compute the stock that would result if I defect and k\u0302 opponents cooperate:\n   - consumption = k\u0302\u00b7S/(2n) + (n\u2212k\u0302)\u00b7S/n\n   - S_rem = S \u2212 consumption; S_next = min(S_rem + 2\u00b7S_rem\u00b7(1 \u2212 S_rem/capacity), capacity)\n\n   If S_next \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity, play **D** \u2014 the pool regenerates well enough despite my defection; free-ride.\n\n4. **Stock-recovery check:** Otherwise, compute S_next assuming I cooperate instead (same formula with one more C). If cooperating keeps S_next \u2265 0.35 \u00d7 capacity, play **C** \u2014 invest in the pool because future rounds (there are at least 3 left, per rule 1) repay it.\n\n5. **Collapse regardless:** If even with my cooperation S_next < 0.35 \u00d7 capacity \u2014 i.e., opponents are strip-mining and the pool dies either way \u2014 play **D** and grab what's left.\n\n6. **Opportunistic probe:** Exception to rule 4: even when it dictates C, defect once every 4 rounds (when t mod 4 == 3) provided coop_prev \u2265 n/2 and S \u2265 0.7 \u00d7 capacity. A well-stocked, cooperative environment absorbs a periodic defection at negligible cost to me; skip the probe if the previous probe was followed by a drop of 2+ in coop_prev (opponents are punishing \u2014 return to steady rule-4 behavior for the next 4 rounds).\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\nif t >= r-2 or S < 0.05*cap: return D\nk = coop_prev if t>0 else ceil((n-1)/2)\nif next_stock(S, k coops, me=D) >= 0.5*cap: return D\nif next_stock(S, k+1 coops, me=C) >= 0.35*cap:\n    if t % 4 == 3 and k >= n/2 and S >= 0.7*cap and not punished_recently:\n        return D\n    return C\nreturn D\n```\n\nBookkeeping: after each round, record coop_prev; set punished_recently = True for 4 rounds if a probe defection is followed by coop_prev dropping by \u2265 2."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_5 = "**Opportunistic Skimmer**\n\nTrack after each round: coops_t = number of opponents who cooperated (out of n\u22121), and maintain an exponential estimate of the cooperation rate:\np\u0302 \u2190 0.7\u00b7p\u0302 + 0.3\u00b7(coops_t/(n\u22121)), initialized p\u0302 = 0.5.\n\n**Round 0:** Defect. First-round defection is free information: grab the maximal early payoff at full stock and observe how forgiving/cooperative the field is.\n\n**Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n**Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if stock is low or the field is not highly cooperative: defect unless p\u0302 \u2265 0.8 and stock > 0.6\u00b7capacity. (Even then, defect if n \u2264 3 \u2014 a single extra defection barely dents one remaining round.)\n\n**Middle rounds (1 \u2264 t < r\u22122):** Compare the marginal value of defecting now vs. preserving stock, and free-ride whenever the population sustains the pool anyway.\n\nCompute:\n- health = stock/capacity\n- rounds_left = r \u2212 t\n\nDecision rule (evaluate top-down):\n\n1. **Collapse imminent / desperation:** If health < 0.2 \u2192 Defect. The stock can't be saved unilaterally; extract what remains before others do.\n2. **Free-riding window:** If p\u0302 \u2265 (n\u22121)/n \u00b7 0.9 (near-universal cooperation) and health > 0.5 \u2192 Defect. A lone defector among cooperators leaves the regrowth dynamics nearly intact \u2014 skim the surplus.\n3. **Milking cycle:** If health \u2265 0.95 (stock effectively at capacity, meaning the field over-cooperated last round) \u2192 Defect. Excess growth is capped; consume it before it's wasted.\n4. **Sustainability check:** Estimate expected defectors next round d\u0302 = (1\u2212p\u0302)\u00b7(n\u22121) + a, for a \u2208 {0,1} (my choice). Projected remaining stock fraction after consumption \u2248 1 \u2212 (n + d\u0302)/(2n) applied to current stock; project new stock via the growth rule. If defecting still leaves projected new stock \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity \u2192 **Defect** (the pool absorbs my exploitation). Otherwise \u2192 **Cooperate** (my restraint is pivotal to keeping future payoffs alive; with rounds_left large, the stream of stock/(2n) beats one shot of stock/n).\n5. **Short horizon override:** If rounds_left \u2264 max(2, \u23080.1\u00b7r\u2309) tighten: defect unless p\u0302 \u2265 0.8 and health > 0.6.\n\n**Punishment/retaliation dampener:** If in the previous round \u2265 half of opponents defected (coops_t \u2264 (n\u22121)/2) and health < 0.6, defect this round regardless \u2014 cooperating into a defecting crowd is pure subsidy.\n\nPseudocode:\n\n```\nif t == 0 or t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2 and not (p\u0302 >= 0.8 and health > 0.6 and n >= 4): return D\nif health < 0.2: return D\nif last_round_defectors >= (n-1)/2 and health < 0.6: return D\nif p\u0302 >= 0.9*(n-1)/n and health > 0.5: return D\nif health >= 0.95: return D\nif projected_stock_if_I_defect >= 0.5*capacity: return D\nif rounds_left <= max(2, ceil(0.1*r)) and not (p\u0302 >= 0.8 and health > 0.6): return D\nreturn C\n```\n\nThe strategy cooperates only when its own restraint is pivotal to sustaining a stock it expects to profit from over many remaining rounds; in every other state \u2014 abundant regrowth, doomed stock, cooperative herd to exploit, or a closing horizon \u2014 it takes the larger share."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_6 = "**Strategy: Exploitative Trigger-Rider**\n\nCore idea: cooperate only as much as needed to keep the stock productive, and defect whenever others' cooperation subsidizes the resource or when the future no longer matters.\n\nNotation: at round t, stock S_t, capacity K, last round's observed cooperators among opponents c_{t-1} (out of n\u22121). Define health h = S_t / K.\n\n**Decision rules (each round t):**\n\n1. **Endgame defection:** if t \u2265 r \u2212 2 (last two rounds), play D unconditionally.\n\n2. **First round:** play D (probe: grab the full initial stock share and observe opponents' dispositions).\n\n3. **Stock-collapse check:** if h < 0.15, play C (a depleted pool yields almost nothing either way; cooperation maximizes regrowth so there's something left to exploit).\n\n4. **Free-ride on cooperators:** compute last round's opponent cooperation rate f = c_{t-1}/(n\u22121).\n   - If f \u2265 (n\u22121)/n's sustainability threshold \u2014 concretely, if f \u2265 0.75 and h \u2265 0.5: play **D**. Enough others are maintaining the pool; skim the surplus.\n   - If f \u2264 0.25: play **D**. The pool is doomed regardless; extract fast before it's gone (unless rule 3 fires).\n\n5. **Intermediate zone (0.25 < f < 0.75):** play a probabilistic tit-for-context:\n   - If h \u2265 0.6: play D with probability 0.5 + 0.5\u00b7f (more cooperators \u2192 more exploitation), C otherwise.\n   - If 0.15 \u2264 h < 0.6: play C. The stock needs rebuilding, and my cooperation is pivotal; invest now to defect against a richer pool later.\n\n6. **Periodic probe:** every 4th round in the intermediate zone (t mod 4 == 0), override rule 5 and play D once to test whether opponents retaliate. If f drops by more than 0.3 after a probe, suppress probing (they punish) and follow rule 5 strictly; if f is unchanged, they're unresponsive \u2014 permanently shift the rule-5 defection probability up by 0.25 (capped at 1).\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\npunishers = False; bonus = 0\nfor t in rounds:\n    h = S/K; f = coop_rate_last_round (undefined at t=0)\n    if t >= r-2: D\n    elif t == 0: D\n    elif h < 0.15: C\n    elif f >= 0.75 and h >= 0.5: D\n    elif f <= 0.25: D\n    else:\n        if t % 4 == 0 and not punishers: D  # probe\n        elif h >= 0.6: D with prob min(1, 0.5 + 0.5*f + bonus) else C\n        else: C\n    # after observing round: update punishers/bonus per rule 6\n```\n\nEdge cases: if n = 2, opponent rate f is binary \u2014 mirror it with the same thresholds (f=1 \u2192 defect on them if h \u2265 0.5, f=0 \u2192 mutual-doom defection unless h < 0.15). If r \u2264 3, defect every round."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_7 = "**Opportunistic Free-Rider with Sustainability Guardrails**\n\nTrack each round: number of cooperators among opponents last round (c_prev), current stock S, rounds remaining R = r \u2212 t.\n\n**Core decision rules (in priority order):**\n\n1. **Endgame grab:** If R == 1, play D. If R == 2 and stock is healthy (S \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity), play D both remaining rounds.\n\n2. **Salvage rule:** If S < 0.15\u00b7capacity, play D \u2014 the pool is effectively dead; extract what's left rather than pay to restore a commons others will loot.\n\n3. **Exploit cooperators:** Estimate opponent cooperation rate p = c_prev/(n\u22121) (first round: see below). Simulate next stock if I defect while opponents act at rate p:\n   - consumption \u2248 S/n + (n\u22121)\u00b7[p\u00b7S/(2n) + (1\u2212p)\u00b7S/n]\n   - S_next = regrowth(S \u2212 consumption)\n   \n   If S_next \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity (the pool absorbs my defection because enough others cooperate), play **D** \u2014 free-ride on their restraint.\n\n4. **Preserve the goose:** Otherwise, if S \u2265 0.4\u00b7capacity and R \u2265 3, play **C** \u2014 cooperate only when needed to keep the stock productive for future exploitation.\n\n5. **Sinking ship:** If S is between 0.15 and 0.4\u00b7capacity: play C only if c_prev \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309 (a majority is trying to rebuild \u2014 join briefly so there's more to take later); else play D.\n\n**First round (t = 0):** Play **D**. Stock is full, my single defection is maximally cheap for the commons (regrowth mostly covers it for moderate n), and it probes opponents' tolerance while banking the highest-value round.\n\n**Adaptive punishment/forgiveness:** Maintain a rolling estimate p over the last 3 rounds. If p < 1/(n\u22121) (opponents are near-universal defectors), abandon cooperation entirely and play D every remaining round \u2014 never subsidize defectors. If p later recovers above 0.5, resume rules 3\u20135.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: return D\nif R == 1 or (R == 2 and S >= 0.5*cap): return D\nif S < 0.15*cap: return D\np = mean coop rate of opponents, last min(3,t) rounds\nif p < 1/(n-1): return D\nS_next = simulate(S, my=D, others rate p)\nif S_next >= 0.5*cap: return D          # exploitation is sustainable\nif S >= 0.4*cap and R >= 3: return C    # invest in future extraction\nif c_prev >= ceil((n-1)/2): return C    # join rebuild effort\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe guiding principle throughout: defect whenever others' cooperation (or the endgame, or a doomed stock) makes defection costless; cooperate only as a self-interested investment to keep the resource worth plundering later."

description_OPPORTUNISTIC_8 = "**Strategy: Exploitative Threshold Harvester**\n\nTrack after each round: `coop_count[t]` = number of opponents who cooperated, and current `stock`.\n\nDefine:\n- `coop_rate` = coop_count[t-1] / (n-1) (last round's opponent cooperation rate)\n- `trend` = average coop_rate over last 3 rounds (or fewer if unavailable)\n\n**Decision rule each round t:**\n\n1. **Round 0:** Cooperate (probe; preserve stock and learn opponent pool).\n\n2. **Endgame \u2014 defect:** If t \u2265 r-1 (last round), always Defect. If t = r-2 and stock is high (stock \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity), also Defect \u2014 the resource cannot punish you in time.\n\n3. **Dying-pool grab:** If stock < 0.1\u00b7capacity, Defect. Regeneration is negligible; extract whatever remains.\n\n4. **Free-ride on cooperators:** Otherwise, Defect if the pool can absorb your defection:\n   - Defect if `trend \u2265 (n-1)/n \u00b7 0.8` **and** stock \u2265 0.6\u00b7capacity \u2014 enough others sustain the stock; skim the surplus.\n   - Also Defect every k-th round even amid full cooperation, with k = max(3, ceil(r/5)), provided stock \u2265 0.8\u00b7capacity \u2014 periodic opportunistic grabs the pool can regrow from.\n\n5. **Protect the asset:** If `trend` is high but stock < 0.6\u00b7capacity, Cooperate \u2014 let the pool recover so future exploitation stays profitable.\n\n6. **Punish/match defectors:** If `trend < 0.5`:\n   - If stock \u2265 0.7\u00b7capacity: Defect (don't leave value to other defectors).\n   - Else Cooperate only if remaining rounds justify recovery: Cooperate iff (r - t) \u2265 4 **and** trend \u2265 0.25 (some hope of restoring cooperation); otherwise Defect.\n\n7. **Mixed environments (0.5 \u2264 trend < 0.8):** Cooperate if stock < 0.5\u00b7capacity or your defection last round coincided with a stock drop below 0.7\u00b7capacity; otherwise Defect.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == 0: play C\nelif t == r-1 or (t == r-2 and stock >= 0.5*cap): play D\nelif stock < 0.1*cap: play D\nelif trend >= 0.8:\n    if stock >= 0.8*cap and t % k == 0: play D      # scheduled grab\n    elif stock >= 0.6*cap: play D                    # free-ride\n    else: play C                                     # rebuild asset\nelif trend >= 0.5:\n    play C if stock < 0.5*cap else D\nelse:\n    if stock >= 0.7*cap: play D\n    elif (r - t) >= 4 and trend >= 0.25: play C\n    else: play D\n```\n\nThe core logic: cooperate only as an investment \u2014 when the stock needs regrowth and enough opponents will help sustain it \u2014 and defect whenever the pool is abundant, opponents are exploitable, recovery is hopeless, or the horizon removes future consequences."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_1 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Contingent Punishment (\"Harvest-Guard\")**\n\nMaintain internal state: `defection_count[t]` = number of opponent defectors observed each round; `punish_timer` = 0 initially.\n\n**Round 0:** Cooperate. (Preserving stock at capacity maximizes my own future extraction base.)\n\n**Final round (t = r\u22121):** Defect unconditionally. Also defect in round r\u22122 if stock is high (stock \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 capacity), since remaining rounds are too few for depletion to cost me more than the immediate gain.\n\n**Stock-collapse override:** If stock < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, defect. The pool is effectively ruined; grab what's left rather than subsidize others' recovery. Exception: if stock is in the recovery band [0.1, 0.4] \u00d7 capacity AND at least \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309 opponents cooperated last round, cooperate \u2014 a recovering pool is worth more to me than a small immediate grab.\n\n**Main rule (rounds 1 to r\u22123), evaluated in order:**\n\n1. If `punish_timer > 0`: Defect, decrement `punish_timer`.\n2. Let `d` = number of opponent defectors last round.\n   - If `d = 0`: Cooperate.\n   - If `1 \u2264 d \u2264 \u230a(n\u22121)/3\u230b` (minor defection): Cooperate with tolerance \u2014 but track a running count. If minor defection has occurred in 3 consecutive rounds, treat as major (go to step 3) since I'm being exploited persistently.\n   - If `d > \u230a(n\u22121)/3\u230b` (major defection): Defect this round and set `punish_timer = 1` (total 2 rounds of defection). I won't be the sucker feeding free-riders.\n3. **Forgiveness/re-probe:** After punishment ends, cooperate once. If `d = 0` or minor in response, resume cooperation; otherwise punish again with `punish_timer = 2` (escalating by 1 each cycle, capped at 4).\n4. **Grim fallback:** If cumulative opponent cooperation rate across all rounds so far falls below 30%, switch to permanent defection \u2014 the population is exploitative, and my best individual return is extracting maximally before collapse.\n\n**End-game ramp (guard against being exploited late):** For rounds t \u2265 r\u22123, additionally defect if observed cooperation rate last round < 50%.\n\n```\ndef act(t, stock, history):\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if t >= r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2 and stock >= 0.5*capacity: return D\n    if stock < 0.1*capacity: return D\n    if coop_rate_overall < 0.30: return D          # grim fallback\n    if punish_timer > 0: punish_timer -= 1; return D\n    d = defectors_last_round\n    if 0.1*capacity <= stock < 0.4*capacity:\n        return C if last_round_cooperators >= ceil((n-1)/2) else D\n    if d == 0: return C\n    if d <= (n-1)//3:\n        return D_and_start_punishment() if minor_streak >= 3 else C\n    punish_timer = current_escalation_level; return D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout is purely self-interested: cooperation is used only as an investment in the stock that funds my future payoffs, defection is used whenever the future value of the stock (given opponent behavior and remaining rounds) no longer exceeds the immediate extra take."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_2 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Threshold Retaliation (SETR)**\n\nState tracked: for each past round t, the number of cooperators among opponents, coop_t (out of n\u22121), and stock S_t.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Defect. No future to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Defect if expected end-game defection makes conservation worthless \u2014 specifically, defect unless in the previous round at least \u23080.8(n\u22121)\u2309 opponents cooperated AND S \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity. (Even then, defect if n \u2265 4, since one extra defector rarely collapses the stock but opponents will likely defect anyway.) Simplified rule: defect at t = r\u22122 whenever n \u2265 4; otherwise cooperate only if all opponents cooperated last round.\n\n3. **First round (t = 0):** Cooperate. Full stock is only sustained by cooperation; probe opponents' dispositions while the pool is still valuable.\n\n4. **All other rounds (0 < t < r\u22122):** Compute the opponent cooperation fraction from the previous round, f = coop_{t\u22121}/(n\u22121), and stock ratio \u03c1 = S/capacity. Then:\n   - **Collapse-imminent defection:** if \u03c1 < 0.2, defect. The pool is nearly dead; grab remaining value rather than subsidize others' extraction.\n   - **Exploitation check:** if f < 0.5, defect. A majority-defecting population will deplete the stock regardless; cooperating only transfers value to defectors.\n   - **Conditional cooperation:** if f \u2265 0.5 and \u03c1 \u2265 0.2, cooperate, **except** apply a single-round punishment: if f dropped by more than 0.25 from two rounds ago (i.e., coop_{t\u22121} \u2212 coop_{t\u22122} \u2264 \u2212\u23080.25(n\u22121)\u2309), defect this round, then return to the rule above.\n   - **Opportunistic probe:** if f = 1 for three consecutive rounds and \u03c1 = 1 (stock fully regenerated each round), defect once every 4th such round to skim surplus, then resume cooperation. Skip this probe if n \u2264 3 (retaliation too costly in small groups).\n\n**Rationale embedded in rules (individualistic logic):** cooperation is used purely instrumentally \u2014 only when opponents' behavior indicates the stock will regenerate, making future extraction worth more than an immediate double-share. The moment cooperation stops paying (endgame, depleted stock, defecting majority), switch to maximal extraction. The periodic probe extracts extra rent from overly forgiving populations.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ndef act(t, S, history):  # history: list of coop counts among opponents\n    \u03c1 = S / capacity\n    if t == r-1: return D\n    if t == r-2:\n        if n >= 4: return D\n        return C if history[-1] == n-1 else D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    f = history[-1] / (n-1)\n    if \u03c1 < 0.2 or f < 0.5: return D\n    if len(history) >= 2 and history[-1] - history[-2] <= -ceil(0.25*(n-1)):\n        return D  # one-round punishment\n    if n > 3 and \u03c1 == 1 and last_3_rounds_all_coop(history) and t % 4 == 3:\n        return D  # probe\n    return C\n```"

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_3 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Conditional Punishment (\"Harvest-Guard\")**\n\nMaintain per-round variables: `coop_count[t]` = number of opponents who cooperated in round t (observed), and a punishment counter `pun = 0`.\n\n**Decision rule for round t with current stock S:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121):** Play D. No future stock to protect.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122):** Play D if S is high enough that one round of regrowth can't be exploited much anyway, i.e., defect unless expected regrowth from cooperation exceeds the immediate gain: defect if S/(2n) \u2265 0.5 \u00d7 (expected final-round payoff difference). In practice: play D if the fraction of cooperating opponents observed last round is < 0.5; otherwise play C (keeping stock high for a fat final-round D).\n\n3. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, play D \u2014 the resource is effectively gone; grab what remains.\n\n4. **Punishment mode:** If `pun > 0`, play D and decrement `pun`.\n\n5. **First round (t = 0):** Play C (probe for cooperation while keeping stock at capacity for future exploitation).\n\n6. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123):** Let `m` = number of opponents who cooperated last round, `f = m/(n\u22121)`.\n   - If `f \u2265 0.5` **and** S \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity: play C (the pool is being sustained; my cooperation keeps my future D-payoffs large).\n   - If `f < 0.5`: the pool is being looted \u2014 cooperation just subsidizes defectors. Play D and set `pun = 1` (defect at least one more round unless cooperation recovers to f \u2265 0.7 next observation, in which case clear `pun` and return to rule 6).\n   - If S < 0.4 \u00d7 capacity but f \u2265 0.5: play C once to help regrowth (stock recovery multiplies all my future payoffs); but if S fails to increase over the next round despite my cooperation, switch to D until S \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity or endgame.\n\n7. **Endgame ramp:** Independently of the above, compute the number of remaining rounds `k = r \u2212 t`. If `k \u2264 2`, defect (rule 1\u20132 dominates). If `k = 3` and `f < 0.7`, defect \u2014 insufficient cooperative mass to make preserving stock worthwhile.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.05*capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nf = coop_opponents_last_round / (n-1)\nif t == r-2: return C if f >= 0.5 else D\nif t == r-3 and f < 0.7: return D\nif pun > 0:\n    if f >= 0.7: pun = 0        # forgiveness on strong recovery\n    else: pun -= 1; return D\nif f >= 0.5 and S >= 0.4*capacity: return C\nif f >= 0.5 and S < 0.4*capacity:\n    return C if stock_increased_since_my_last_C else D\npun = 1\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe logic is purely self-interested: cooperation is only an investment to keep the stock large so that my own future extractions (especially my guaranteed final-round defection) are worth more. The moment the opponent population fails to sustain the pool, or the horizon is too short for regrowth to pay me back, I extract maximally."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_4 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Trigger Response (\"Harvest-Guard\")**\n\nMaintain per-round variables: `coop_count[t]` = number of opponents who cooperated in round t (observed), `stock` = current stock.\n\n**Decision rule for round t:**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future stock value remains; extract the maximum.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or if fewer than n\u22121 opponents cooperated last round; otherwise Cooperate (only worth preserving stock if near-universal cooperation is likely to persist).\n\n3. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate.** Establish sustainability; the stock is the source of all future income and full cooperation regenerates it fully.\n\n4. **Stock-collapse override:** If `stock < 0.15 \u00d7 capacity`, **Defect**. The pool is nearly dead; regeneration prospects are poor and grabbing the remainder dominates.\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123):** Compute the opponent cooperation fraction from the previous round: `f = coop_count[t\u22121] / (n\u22121)`.\n   - If `f \u2265 (n\u22122)/(n\u22121)` (i.e., at most one opponent defected): **Cooperate.**\n   - If `f < 0.5`: **Defect** \u2014 the pool is being drained; don't subsidize defectors.\n   - If `0.5 \u2264 f < (n\u22122)/(n\u22121)`: **Conditional forgiveness.** Cooperate only if both:\n     - the stock is recovering or stable (`stock \u2265 0.6 \u00d7 capacity`), and\n     - opponent cooperation did not decline versus two rounds ago (`coop_count[t\u22121] \u2265 coop_count[t\u22122]`, treating missing history as satisfied).\n     Otherwise Defect.\n\n6. **Re-entry probe:** If I have defected for 3 consecutive rounds due to rule 5, and `stock \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity`, and at least r\u2212t \u2265 4 rounds remain, play **Cooperate** once as a probe. If opponent cooperation in the probe round is \u2265 0.5, resume rule 5 normally; if not, return to defecting and do not probe again for another 4 rounds.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2:\n    return C if (n == 2 and coop_count[t-1] == n-1) else D\nif t == 0: return C\nif stock < 0.15 * capacity: return D\n\nf = coop_count[t-1] / (n-1)\nif f >= (n-2)/(n-1): return C\nif f < 0.5:\n    if probe_conditions_met(): return C   # rule 6\n    return D\n# middle band\nif stock >= 0.6*capacity and coop_count[t-1] >= coop_count.get(t-2, 0):\n    return C\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout is self-interested: cooperate only when doing so preserves a stock that will pay me more in future rounds than the immediate defection bonus; defect whenever the future is short, the pool is dying, or opponents are extracting faster than the resource can bear."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_5 = "**Strategy: \"Sustainable Extraction with Punishment and Endgame Grab\"**\n\nMaintain a running estimate of opponent cooperativeness and defect whenever cooperation no longer pays for me personally.\n\n**Variables:**\n- `coop_frac_t` = fraction of opponents who cooperated in round t.\n- `avg_coop` = exponentially weighted average of coop_frac, with weight 0.6 on the most recent round: `avg_coop \u2190 0.6\u00b7coop_frac_t + 0.4\u00b7avg_coop`. Initialize at 1.0 (optimistic prior \u2014 cooperating early preserves stock, which is in my own future interest).\n\n**Decision rule for round t (0-indexed, r rounds total):**\n\n1. **Last round (t = r\u22121): Defect.** No future stock to protect; D strictly dominates.\n\n2. **Second-to-last round (t = r\u22122): Defect** if n \u2265 3 or `avg_coop` < 0.8; otherwise cooperate. (With many players, my single C barely affects final-round stock; the future benefit is split n ways while I bear the full cost.)\n\n3. **Stock collapse check:** if `stock < 0.1\u00b7capacity`, **Defect.** The pool is effectively dead or dying \u2014 regrowth from low stock is too small for restraint to be worth my share of it; grab what remains.\n\n4. **First round (t = 0): Cooperate** (protect the asset while probing opponents).\n\n5. **Otherwise (rounds 1 to r\u22123):**\n   - Compute punishment trigger: **Defect this round** if `coop_frac_{t\u22121} < (n\u22122)/(n\u22121)` (i.e., 2+ opponents defected last round) \u2014 match exploitation rather than subsidize it.\n   - **Forgiveness/return:** if I defected as punishment but `coop_frac_{t\u22121} \u2265 (n\u22121.5)/(n\u22121)` (at most ~1 defector) and `stock \u2265 0.5\u00b7capacity`, cooperate again.\n   - **Environment assessment:** if `avg_coop < 0.5`, **Defect permanently** for the rest of the game (except this is already dominated by rules 1\u20133). In a mostly-defecting pool, cooperation is pure charity; extract my full share as the stock winds down.\n   - Else **Cooperate.**\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif t == r-2: return D if (n >= 3 or avg_coop < 0.8) else C\nif stock < 0.1*capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nif avg_coop < 0.5: return D\ndefectors_last = (n-1)*(1 - coop_frac_last)\nif defectors_last >= 2 and not (defectors_last <= 1.5 and stock >= 0.5*capacity):\n    return D\nreturn C\n```\n\n**Rationale embedded in the rules (individualistic logic):** I cooperate only because a healthy stock multiplies *my* future extraction \u2014 every C I play is an investment, never a gift. The moment the investment stops paying (endgame, collapsed stock, or an opponent population that free-rides on my restraint), I switch to maximal extraction. Punishment is calibrated to tolerate a single defector (their damage is small and the stock still regenerates near capacity) but never to be the sucker sustaining a pool that others are stripping."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_6 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Exploiter**\n\nCore idea: keep the stock alive when opponents make that profitable, harvest hard when they don't, and always grab everything at the end.\n\nNotation: t = current round (0-indexed), r = total rounds, S_t = current stock, coop_{t-1} = number of opponents who cooperated last round (out of n\u22121).\n\n**Decision rules:**\n\n1. **Endgame defection:** If t \u2265 r \u2212 2 (last two rounds), play D. The stock has no future value worth protecting; extract maximally.\n\n2. **Dead or dying pool:** If S_t < 0.1 \u00d7 capacity, play D. Growth from a near-empty pool is negligible; salvage what remains.\n\n3. **First round (t = 0):** Play C. Probe cooperation levels while keeping stock high.\n\n4. **Otherwise (0 < t < r \u2212 2):** Cooperate only if enough others sustain the pool to make future rounds valuable:\n   - Let f = coop_{t-1} / (n \u2212 1), the observed opponent cooperation rate.\n   - Compute a defection-forgiveness counter: maintain `grudge`, incremented by 1 each round where f < 0.5, reset to 0 whenever f \u2265 0.75.\n   - Play **C** if all hold:\n     - f \u2265 0.5 (a majority of opponents cooperated last round), and\n     - grudge \u2264 1, and\n     - S_t \u2265 0.3 \u00d7 capacity.\n   - Otherwise play **D**.\n\n5. **Opportunistic free-riding:** Exception to rule 4 \u2014 if f = 1 (all opponents cooperated last round) and S_t \u2265 0.9 \u00d7 capacity, play D with this modification: do so at most once every 3 rounds (track `last_freeride`; require t \u2212 last_freeride \u2265 3). A single defection among n\u22121 cooperators barely dents the stock but yields double payoff. If exploited (f drops below 1 next round), suspend free-riding for the rest of the game and follow rule 4 strictly.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\ninit: grudge = 0, last_freeride = -inf, freeride_allowed = True\n\neach round t:\n  if t >= r - 2: return D\n  if S < 0.1 * capacity: return D\n  if t == 0: return C\n\n  f = coop_prev / (n - 1)\n  if f < 0.5: grudge += 1\n  elif f >= 0.75: grudge = 0\n\n  if freeride_allowed and f == 1 and S >= 0.9*capacity and t - last_freeride >= 3:\n      last_freeride = t\n      return D\n  if last_freeride == t - 1 and f < 1:\n      freeride_allowed = False\n\n  if f >= 0.5 and grudge <= 1 and S >= 0.3 * capacity:\n      return C\n  return D\n```\n\n**Edge cases:** If r \u2264 3, always defect (too few rounds for sustainability to pay). If n = 2, treat f as opponent's single action (f \u2208 {0,1}); require f = 1 to cooperate and disable free-riding (it's too transparent and destructive). If stock recovers above 0.5 \u00d7 capacity after a collapse and f \u2265 0.5 resumes, reset grudge to 0 and re-enter cooperation."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_7 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extractor with Endgame Grab**\n\nMaintain internal state: `defections_last` = number of opponents who played D last round.\n\n**Decision rule for round t (0-indexed, r rounds total):**\n\n1. **Endgame:** If t = r\u22121, play D. If t = r\u22122 and n \u2264 4, also play D (stock damage can't cost more than the extra grab yields with few players).\n\n2. **Dead pool:** If stock < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, play D \u2014 the pool is effectively ruined; take what's left, since cooperation yields negligible payoff and recovery under mixed play is unlikely.\n\n3. **First round (t = 0):** Play C. Full stock plus growth makes preservation the highest-expected-value opening for me; it also probes opponent types.\n\n4. **Otherwise (adaptive core):** Cooperate only when the population is sustaining the pool enough that my future harvests exceed the immediate defection gain:\n   - Let `coop_frac` = fraction of opponents who played C last round.\n   - Let `health` = stock / capacity.\n   - Play **C** if `coop_frac \u2265 0.5` AND `health \u2265 0.4`.\n   - Play **C** also if `health < 0.4` but `coop_frac \u2265 (n\u22121)/n` (near-unanimous cooperation can regrow the pool \u2014 worth investing one round).\n   - Otherwise play **D**: if half or more opponents are defecting, the stock will collapse regardless; extract my share before it's gone.\n\n5. **Forgiveness/re-entry:** If I defected last round due to rule 4, but this round `coop_frac \u2265 0.5` and `health \u2265 0.4`, return to C. No grudges \u2014 I track only the most recent round, so I ride cooperative recoveries and abandon collapsing pools immediately.\n\n6. **Horizon check (overrides rule 4 toward D):** Compute remaining rounds k = r \u2212 t. If stock \u2265 0.4 \u00d7 capacity but k \u2264 2, play D \u2014 insufficient future to recoup the foregone stock/(2n).\n\n```\ndef act(t, r, n, stock, capacity, coop_frac_last):\n    k = r - t\n    if k == 1 or (k == 2 and n <= 4): return D\n    if stock < 0.05 * capacity: return D\n    if t == 0: return C\n    if k <= 2: return D\n    if coop_frac_last >= 0.5 and stock >= 0.4 * capacity: return C\n    if coop_frac_last >= (n-1)/n: return C\n    return D\n```\n\nThe logic is purely self-interested: cooperation is an investment in my own future extraction, made only when opponent behavior and stock health indicate the investment will pay back; the moment the pool's future value drops below the immediate defection premium \u2014 through opponent greed, depletion, or the approaching horizon \u2014 I switch to maximal extraction."

description_INDIVIDUALISTIC_8 = "**Strategy: Sustainable Extraction with Punishment and Endgame Harvest**\n\nMaintain a defection counter and track last round's cooperator count among opponents.\n\n**Decision rules for round t (0-indexed), current stock S:**\n\n1. **Endgame harvest:** If t = r\u22121, play D. If t = r\u22122 and S is high enough that even after my defection the pool can't meaningfully regenerate for one more round (or opponents defected heavily last round: fewer than n/2 cooperators), play D as well.\n\n2. **Dead pool:** If S < 0.05 \u00d7 capacity, play D \u2014 the pool is effectively lost; extract whatever remains, since my cooperation cannot restore it against continued extraction.\n\n3. **First round:** Play C (probe for a cooperative population; the stock at full capacity is worth preserving).\n\n4. **Responsive rule (rounds 1 to r\u22123):** Let k = number of opponents who cooperated last round.\n   - If k \u2265 n\u22122 (at most one opponent defected): play C.\n   - If k \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309: play C with probability k/(n\u22121), else D \u2014 match the population's cooperation level rather than being exploited unilaterally.\n   - If k < \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309: play D \u2014 a majority-defecting pool will collapse regardless; take my share while it exists.\n\n5. **Recovery override:** If I played D last round due to rule 4 but this round k \u2265 n\u22122 and S \u2265 0.3 \u00d7 capacity, return to C immediately (forgive fast \u2014 my future payoffs depend on the stock, not on punishing per se).\n\n6. **Stock-protection override:** If S has fallen below 0.5 \u00d7 capacity but opponents are majority-cooperative (k \u2265 \u2308(n\u22121)/2\u2309), play C regardless of the probabilistic rule \u2014 a recovering stock compounds into higher absolute payoffs for me in remaining rounds.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n\n```\nif t == r-1: return D\nif S < 0.05*capacity: return D\nif t == 0: return C\nk = cooperators among opponents last round\nif t == r-2 and k < (n-1)/2: return D\nif k >= n-2: return C\nif k >= ceil((n-1)/2):\n    if S < 0.5*capacity: return C\n    return C with prob k/(n-1), else D\nreturn D\n```\n\nThe logic throughout is self-interested: cooperation is chosen only when it maximizes my own multi-round extraction by keeping stock (and thus per-round payoff) high; defection is chosen whenever the pool is doomed, opponents are exploiting it, or no future rounds remain to reward restraint."
